1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,575
Chapter 40: Preeclampsia Syndrome.

3
00:00:05,575 --> 00:00:22,662
Terminology And Diagnosis Incidence And Risk Factors Etiopathogenesis Pathophysiology Prediction Prevention References Hypertensive disorders include preeclampsia, gestational hypertension, and chronic hypertension and complicate up to 10 percent of pregnancies.

4
00:00:22,662 --> 00:00:30,887
As a group, they are one member of the deadly triad—along with hemorrhage and infection—that contributes greatly to maternal morbidity .

5
00:00:30,887 --> 00:00:37,275
Preeclampsia, either alone or superimposed on chronic hypertension, is the most dangerous.

6
00:00:37,275 --> 00:00:47,437
In the United States from 2011 to 2015, 7 percent of pregnancy-related maternal deaths were caused by preeclampsia or eclampsia .

7
00:00:47,437 --> 00:00:51,400
Most hypertension-related deaths are deemed preventable .

8
00:00:51,400 --> 00:01:01,025
In response, Joint Commission (2019) accredited hospitals are now required to track their recognition and timely treatment of hypertension.

9
00:01:01,025 --> 00:01:08,962
In 2018, a workshop to study preeclampsia was convened by the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute.

10
00:01:08,962 --> 00:01:18,600
This builds on the prior work of the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists’ Task Force on Hypertension in Pregnancy (2013).

11
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:25,912
Its purpose was to review topics regarding all aspects of preeclampsia and to recommend future research areas.

12
00:01:25,912 --> 00:01:31,012
Many of these topics are discussed throughout this chapter and Chapter 41.

13
00:01:31,012 --> 00:01:33,750
Terminology And Diagnosis.

14
00:01:33,750 --> 00:01:47,812
To codify the classification of hypertensive disorders of pregnancy, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2013, 2020) describes four types of hypertensive disease: 1.

15
00:01:47,812 --> 00:01:51,250
Preeclampsia and eclampsia syndrome 2.

16
00:01:51,250 --> 00:01:54,787
Chronic hypertension of any etiology 3.

17
00:01:54,787 --> 00:01:59,075
Preeclampsia superimposed on chronic hypertension 4.

18
00:01:59,075 --> 00:02:08,650
Gestational hypertension, in which definitive evidence for the preeclampsia syndrome does not develop and hypertension resolves by 12 weeks postpartum.

19
00:02:08,650 --> 00:02:17,450
This classification aims to differentiate preeclampsia syndrome, which is potentially more ominous, from other hypertensive disorders.

20
00:02:17,450 --> 00:02:21,187
# Diagnosis of Hypertensive Disorders.

21
00:02:21,187 --> 00:02:32,187
Hypertension is diagnosed empirically when systolic and diastolic blood pressures exceed 140 mm Hg and 90 mm Hg, respectively.

22
00:02:32,187 --> 00:02:36,512
Korotkoff phase V is used to define diastolic pressure.

23
00:02:36,512 --> 00:02:54,912
Previously for pregnant women, increases of 30 mm Hg systolic or 15 mm Hg diastolic above blood pressure values taken at midpregnancy had also been used as diagnostic criteria, even when absolute values were <140/90 mm Hg.

24
00:02:54,912 --> 00:02:59,687
These incremental changes are no longer used to define hypertension.

25
00:02:59,687 --> 00:03:10,750
However, blood pressure surveillance in these gravidas is reasonable because eclamptic seizures develop in some whose blood pressures have stayed below 140/90 mm Hg .

26
00:03:10,750 --> 00:03:24,375
In other cases, mean arterial pressures that suddenly rise but that still lie in normal range—“delta hypertension”—may signify preeclampsia (Macdonald-Wallis, 2012; Zeeman, 2007).

27
00:03:24,375 --> 00:03:34,987
We use this term to describe a relatively acute rise in blood pressure in individual patients, albeit some still with pressures <140/90 mm Hg .

28
00:03:34,987 --> 00:03:47,812
Some of these women will go on to have obvious preeclampsia, and some even develop eclamptic seizures or hemolysis, elevated liver enzyme levels, and low platelet count (HELLP) syndrome.

29
00:03:47,812 --> 00:03:50,125
Figure 40-1.

30
00:03:50,125 --> 00:03:56,387
Schematic of normal reference ranges for mean arterial blood pressure changes across pregnancy.

31
00:03:56,387 --> 00:04:02,425
Patient A (blue) has mean blood pressures near the 20th percentile throughout pregnancy.

32
00:04:02,425 --> 00:04:13,862
Patient B (red) has a similar pattern with mean pressures at the 25th percentile until approximately 36 weeks’ gestation, at which time her blood pressure begins to rise.

33
00:04:13,862 --> 00:04:21,125
By term, it is substantively higher and in the 75th percentile, but she is still considered “normotensive.

34
00:04:21,125 --> 00:04:27,175
This increase from the 25th to 75th percentile can be termed “delta hypertension.

35
00:04:27,175 --> 00:04:40,125
Historically, systolic and diastolic blood pressure levels of 140/90 mm Hg have been arbitrarily used since the 1950s to define “hypertension” in nonpregnant individuals.

36
00:04:40,125 --> 00:04:47,237
However, these levels were selected by insurance companies to characterize a population of middle-aged men.

37
00:04:47,237 --> 00:04:53,100
It seems more realistic to define normal-range blood pressures for specific populations—

38
00:04:53,260 --> 00:04:59,822
such as young, healthy, pregnant women (Lu, 2019; Reddy, 2020).

39
00:04:59,772 --> 00:05:06,672
To provide such data, >1000 women were recently studied longitudinally through pregnancy .

40
00:05:06,672 --> 00:05:10,147
Data of this type may shape future thresholds.

41
00:05:10,147 --> 00:05:13,185
# Gestational Hypertension.

42
00:05:13,185 --> 00:05:23,760
Women with gestational hypertension have blood pressures that reach 140/90 mm Hg or greater for the first time after midpregnancy but lack proteinuria.

43
00:05:23,760 --> 00:05:28,447
Almost half of affected women subsequently develop preeclampsia .

44
00:05:28,447 --> 00:05:38,660
Even so, when blood pressure rises appreciably, it is dangerous to both mother and fetus to ignore this elevation only because proteinuria has not yet developed .

45
00:05:38,660 --> 00:05:47,247
As Chesley (1985) emphasized, 10 percent of eclamptic seizures develop before overt proteinuria can be detected.

46
00:05:47,247 --> 00:05:57,847
Gestational hypertension is reclassified by some as transient hypertension if preeclampsia does not develop and blood pressure returns to normal by 12 weeks postpartum.

47
00:05:57,847 --> 00:06:00,735
# Preeclampsia Syndrome.

48
00:06:00,735 --> 00:06:07,735
Preeclampsia is best described as a pregnancy-specific syndrome that can affect virtually every organ system.

49
00:06:07,735 --> 00:06:17,172
Although preeclampsia is more than simply gestational hypertension with proteinuria, the appearance of protein remains a primary diagnostic criterion.

50
00:06:17,172 --> 00:06:24,447
It is an objective marker and reflects the system-wide endothelial leak that characterizes the preeclampsia syndrome.

51
00:06:24,447 --> 00:06:45,047
Last, preeclampsia can be divided into early onset, <34 weeks; late onset, ≥34 weeks; preterm onset, <37 weeks; and term onset, ≥37 weeks (Burton, 2019; Poon, 2019).

52
00:06:45,047 --> 00:06:51,697
In some women with preeclampsia, neither overt proteinuria nor fetalgrowth restriction are features .

53
00:06:51,697 --> 00:07:00,935
Because of this, the Task Force (2013) suggests other diagnostic criteria, some of which are shown in Table 40-1.

54
00:07:00,935 --> 00:07:12,285
Multiorgan involvement may be reflected by thrombocytopenia, renal dysfunction, hepatocellular necrosis, central nervous system perturbations, or pulmonary edema.

55
00:07:12,285 --> 00:07:14,535
Table 40-1.

56
00:07:14,535 --> 00:07:24,997
Classification and Diagnosis of Pregnancy-Associated Hypertension The markers listed in Table 40-1 help also to classify preeclampsia syndrome severity.

57
00:07:24,997 --> 00:07:34,722
Although many use a dichotomous “mild” and “severe” classification, the Task Force (2013) discourages the use of “mild preeclampsia.

58
00:07:34,722 --> 00:07:51,285
It is problematic that there are criteria for the diagnosis of “severe” preeclampsia, but the binary default classification is either implied or specifically termed “mild,” “less severe,” or “nonsevere” (Alexander, 2003; Lindheimer, 2008).

59
00:07:51,285 --> 00:07:57,735
No consensus criteria define “moderate” preeclampsia, which is an elusive third category.

60
00:07:57,735 --> 00:08:10,660
We use the criteria recommended by the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020), some of which are listed in Table 40-2 and categorize disease as “severe” versus “nonsevere.

61
00:08:10,660 --> 00:08:12,972
Table 40-2.

62
00:08:12,972 --> 00:08:19,660
Indicators of Severity of Gestational Hypertensive Disordersa Some symptoms are considered ominous.

63
00:08:19,660 --> 00:08:28,622
Headaches or visual disturbances can precede eclampsia, which is a convulsion in a woman with preeclampsia that is not attributable to another cause.

64
00:08:28,622 --> 00:08:33,810
The seizures are generalized and may appear before, during, or after labor.

65
00:08:33,810 --> 00:08:44,085
The proportion that develops seizures later—after 48 hours postpartum—approximates 10 percent (Sibai, 2005; Zwart, 2008).

66
00:08:44,085 --> 00:08:53,497
Another symptom, epigastric or right upper quadrant pain, frequently accompanies hepatocellular necrosis, ischemia, and hepatic edema.

67
00:08:53,497 --> 00:08:58,085
Elevated serum hepatic transaminase levels can be one marker.

68
00:08:58,085 --> 00:09:03,272
Last, thrombocytopenia also signifies worsening preeclampsia.

69
00:09:03,272 --> 00:09:09,285
It represents platelet activation and aggregation and microangiopathic hemolysis.

70
00:09:09,285 --> 00:09:15,235
Other factors indicative of severe preeclampsia include renal or cardiac involvement.

71
00:09:15,235 --> 00:09:22,597
When these signs and symptoms are profound, they likely cannot be temporized, and delivery will more likely be required.

72
00:09:22,597 --> 00:09:34,560
Importantly, differentiating nonsevere and severe gestational hypertension or preeclampsia can be misleading because what might be apparently mild disease may progress rapidly to severe disease.

73
00:09:34,560 --> 00:09:39,172
# Preeclampsia Superimposed on Chronic Hypertension.

74
00:09:39,172 --> 00:09:45,910
Any chronic hypertensive disorder predisposes a woman to develop superimposed preeclampsia syndrome.

75
00:09:45,910 --> 00:09:47,447
Chronic

76
00:09:47,596 --> 00:09:59,933
underlying hypertension is diagnosed in women with documented blood pressures ≥140/90 mm Hg before pregnancy or before 20 weeks’ gestation, or both.

77
00:09:59,883 --> 00:10:07,621
Thus, in women who are not first seen until after midpregnancy, hypertensive disorders can be difficult to classify.

78
00:10:07,621 --> 00:10:17,296
For example, a woman with previously undiagnosed chronic vascular disease who is seen before 20 weeks frequently has blood pressures within normal range.

79
00:10:17,296 --> 00:10:29,046
During the third trimester, however, as blood pressures return to their originally hypertensive levels, it may be difficult to determine whether hypertension is chronic or induced by pregnancy.

80
00:10:29,046 --> 00:10:42,646
Even a careful search for evidence of preexisting end-organ damage may be futile, as many of these women have mild disease and no evidence of ventricular hypertrophy, retinal vascular changes, or renal involvement.

81
00:10:42,646 --> 00:10:52,658
In 20 to 50 percent of women with chronic hypertension, blood pressure rises to obviously abnormal levels, typically after 24 weeks’ gestation.

82
00:10:52,658 --> 00:11:04,083
If new-onset or worsening baseline hypertension is accompanied by new-onset proteinuria or other findings listed in Table 40-1, superimposed preeclampsia is diagnosed .

83
00:11:04,083 --> 00:11:11,058
Compared with “pure” preeclampsia, superimposed preeclampsia commonly develops earlier in pregnancy.

84
00:11:11,058 --> 00:11:16,396
It tends to be more severe and more often is accompanied by fetal-growth restriction.

85
00:11:16,396 --> 00:11:24,083
The same criteria shown in Table 40-2 also further characterize the severity of superimposed preeclampsia.

86
00:11:24,083 --> 00:11:26,571
Incidence And Risk Factors.

87
00:11:26,571 --> 00:11:35,396
Preeclampsia is identified in 5 to 8 percent of all pregnancies (Jim, 2017; Poon, 2019).

88
00:11:35,396 --> 00:11:44,733
Young and nulliparous women are particularly vulnerable, whereas older women are at greater risk for chronic hypertension with superimposed preeclampsia .

89
00:11:44,733 --> 00:11:51,908
In one review of global studies, the incidence of preeclampsia in nulliparas ranged from 3 to 10 percent .

90
00:11:51,908 --> 00:12:00,158
In multiparas, the incidence ranges from 2 to 5 percent (Jim, 2017; Poon, 2019).

91
00:12:00,158 --> 00:12:07,171
The incidence of preeclampsia is also influenced by race, ethnicity, and genetic predisposition.

92
00:12:07,171 --> 00:12:23,083
In one study by the Maternal–Fetal Medicine Units (MFMU) Network, the incidence of preeclampsia was 5 percent in white, 9 percent in Hispanic, and 11 percent in African American nulliparas (Myatt, 2012a,b).

93
00:12:23,083 --> 00:12:28,983
In addition, black women carry higher risk for associated severe adverse outcomes .

94
00:12:28,983 --> 00:12:39,721
For several clinical factors, Bartsch and associates (2016) extracted data from more than 25 million pregnancies and calculated relative risks .

95
00:12:39,721 --> 00:12:46,933
Major risks include older age, nulliparity, obesity, diabetes, and chronic hypertension.

96
00:12:46,933 --> 00:12:54,533
Another is preeclampsia and especially HELLP syndrome in a prior pregnancy (Malström, 2020).

97
00:12:54,533 --> 00:13:04,883
Underlying metabolic syndrome, hyperhomocysteinemia, or chronic kidney disease are others (Masoudian, 2016; Wiles, 2020).

98
00:13:04,883 --> 00:13:07,183
Table 40-3.

99
00:13:07,183 --> 00:13:26,958
Selected Clinical Risk Factors for Preeclampsia Of lesser factors, human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) seropositivity, sleep-disordered breathing, and a male fetus pose a slightly higher risk (Facco, 2017; Jaskolka, 2017; Sansone, 2016).

100
00:13:26,958 --> 00:13:40,121
Previously affected family members are another, and maternal and fetal genetics are assuming greater predictive importance (Burton, 2019; Gray, 2018; Phipps, 2019).

101
00:13:40,121 --> 00:13:44,821
Last, preeclampsia frequently complicates the “mirror syndrome” .

102
00:13:44,821 --> 00:13:54,096
Although smoking during pregnancy causes various adverse pregnancy outcomes, ironically, it lowers the risk for hypertension during pregnancy.

103
00:13:54,096 --> 00:14:00,371
For eclampsia, seizure incidence has declined in areas where health care is more readily available.

104
00:14:00,371 --> 00:14:13,571
In countries with adequate resources, the incidence averages 1 case in 2000 to 3000 deliveries (Jaatinen, 2016; O’Connor, 2013; Schaap, 2019).

105
00:14:13,571 --> 00:14:21,858
At Parkland Hospital, the incidence has declined appreciably during the past decade and approximates 1 case in 2000 births .

106
00:14:21,858 --> 00:14:28,496
This frequency may be related to improved access to prenatal care and our active management approach .

107
00:14:28,496 --> 00:14:30,783
Figure 40-2.

108
00:14:30,783 --> 00:14:38,633
Incidence of eclampsia per 10,000 deliveries at Parkland Hospital (p <0.05 for trend).

109
00:14:38,633 --> 00:14:40,971
Etiopathogenesis.

110
00:14:40,971 --> 00:14:46,858
The mechanisms by which pregnancy incites or aggravates hypertension remain unsolved.

111
00:14:46,858 --> 00:14:50,058
Any satisfactory theory concerning the

112
00:14:50,212 --> 00:15:16,962
origins of preeclampsia must account for the observation that gestational hypertensive disorders are more likely to develop in women with the following characteristics: • Exposure to chorionic villi for the first time • Exposure to a superabundance of chorionic villi, as with twins or hydatidiform mole • Preexisting conditions associated with endothelial cell activation or inflammation • Genetic predisposition to hypertension developing during pregnancy.

113
00:15:16,962 --> 00:15:21,112
A fetus is not a requisite for preeclampsia to develop.

114
00:15:21,112 --> 00:15:25,849
Although chorionic villi are essential, they need not be intrauterine.

115
00:15:25,849 --> 00:15:31,387
For example, preeclampsia can develop with an advanced abdominal pregnancy .

116
00:15:31,387 --> 00:15:44,624
Regardless of precipitating etiology, the cascade of promoting events leads to systemic vascular endothelial damage, vasospasm, plasma transudation, and ischemic and thrombotic sequelae.

117
00:15:44,624 --> 00:15:47,462
# Phenotypic Expression.

118
00:15:47,462 --> 00:15:57,474
This varies widely for preeclampsia, and phenotype is affected by the degree of remodeling of uterine spiral arterioles by endovascular trophoblasts.

119
00:15:57,474 --> 00:16:03,362
This process underlies the “two-stage disorder” theory of preeclampsia pathogenesis.

120
00:16:03,362 --> 00:16:16,674
According to Redman and coworkers (2015), stage I—the placental syndrome—is caused by faulty endovascular trophoblastic remodeling that downstream causes stage II—the maternal syndrome.

121
00:16:16,674 --> 00:16:25,349
Importantly, stage II can be modified by maternal conditions that also manifest endothelial cell activation or inflammation.

122
00:16:25,349 --> 00:16:33,974
These include chronic hypertension, renal disease, obesity, immunological or connective tissue disorders, and diabetes.

123
00:16:33,974 --> 00:16:39,999
Such staging is artificial, and preeclampsia syndrome presents a spectrum of disease .

124
00:16:39,999 --> 00:16:44,937
Moreover, “isoforms” likely exist and are discussed subsequently.

125
00:16:44,937 --> 00:16:59,349
Differences include maternal and fetal characteristics, placental findings, genetic factors, and early- versus lateonset disease (Gray, 2018; Phipps, 2019; Poon, 2019).

126
00:16:59,349 --> 00:17:01,662
# Etiology.

127
00:17:01,662 --> 00:17:27,924
Of suggested mechanisms to explain the cause of preeclampsia, primary ones include: • Placental implantation with abnormal trophoblastic invasion of uterine vessels • Dysfunctional immunological tolerance between maternal, paternal (placental), and fetal tissues • Maternal maladaptation to cardiovascular or inflammatory changes of normal pregnancy • Genetic factors that include predisposing genes and epigenetic influences.

128
00:17:27,924 --> 00:17:31,274
# Stage I—Placental Syndrome.

129
00:17:31,274 --> 00:17:42,812
Normal placental implantation, as discussed in Chapter 5 (p. 90), is characterized by extensive remodeling of the spiral arterioles within the decidua basalis .

130
00:17:42,812 --> 00:17:50,312
In this “placental bed,” endovascular trophoblasts replace the vascular endothelial and muscular linings.

131
00:17:50,312 --> 00:17:54,187
This advantageously enlarges arteriole diameter .

132
00:17:54,187 --> 00:17:57,337
Veins are invaded only superficially.

133
00:17:57,337 --> 00:18:03,599
In some preeclampsia cases, but not all, trophoblastic invasion may be incomplete.

134
00:18:03,599 --> 00:18:11,049
With this, decidual vessels, but not myometrial vessels, become lined with endovascular trophoblasts.

135
00:18:11,049 --> 00:18:17,849
The deeper myometrial arterioles thus do not lose their endothelial lining and musculoelastic tissue.

136
00:18:17,849 --> 00:18:24,999
As a result, their mean external diameter is only half that of corresponding vessels in normal placentas .

137
00:18:24,999 --> 00:18:30,037
This mechanism is more prevalent in women with early-onset preeclampsia .

138
00:18:30,037 --> 00:18:37,837
Evidence suggests a critical role for soluble antiangiogenic growth factors in this faulty endovascular remodeling .

139
00:18:37,837 --> 00:18:40,212
Figure 40-3.

140
00:18:40,212 --> 00:18:48,624
Schematic representation of normal placental implantation shows proliferation of extravillous trophoblasts from an anchoring villus.

141
00:18:48,624 --> 00:18:59,187
These trophoblasts invade the decidua and extend into the walls of the spiral arteriole to replace the endothelium and muscular wall to create a dilated lowresistance vessel.

142
00:18:59,187 --> 00:19:09,124
With preeclampsia, there is defective implantation characterized by incomplete invasion of the spiral arteriolar wall by extravillous trophoblasts.

143
00:19:09,124 --> 00:19:13,724
This results in a small-caliber vessel with high resistance to flow.

144
00:19:13,724 --> 00:19:28,012
From placental electron microscopy studies, early preeclamptic changes include endothelial damage, insudation of plasma constituents into vessel walls, myointimal cell proliferation, and medial necrosis .

145
00:19:28,012 --> 00:19:31,799
Hertig (1945) referred to lipid

146
00:19:31,948 --> 00:19:37,098
accumulation in myointimal cells and macrophages as atherosis.

147
00:19:37,048 --> 00:19:44,523
These findings are more common in placentas from women diagnosed with preeclampsia before 34 weeks’ gestation .

148
00:19:44,523 --> 00:19:54,485
Acute placental vascular atherosis may also identify a group of women at greater risk for atherosclerosis and cardiovascular disease later in life .

149
00:19:54,485 --> 00:20:04,448
In pregnancy, abnormally narrow spiral arteriole lumens likely impair placental blood flow, reduce perfusion, and create a hypoxic environment .

150
00:20:04,448 --> 00:20:12,035
At this point, these changes incite a systemic inflammatory response, which is stage II or the maternal syndrome.

151
00:20:12,035 --> 00:20:31,073
Defective placentation is posited to further predispose affected women to gestational hypertension, preeclampsia syndrome, preterm delivery, fetal growth-restriction, and placental abruption (Brosens, 2019; Labarrere, 2017; Nelson, 2014).

152
00:20:31,073 --> 00:20:40,673
Immunological Factors Maternal immune tolerance to paternally derived placental and fetal antigens is discussed in Chapter 5 (p. 85).

153
00:20:40,673 --> 00:20:45,110
Loss of this tolerance is another cited theory for preeclampsia .

154
00:20:45,110 --> 00:20:53,423
Certainly, histological changes at the maternal–placental interface in those with preeclampsia suggest acute graft rejection.

155
00:20:53,423 --> 00:21:00,435
Tolerance dysregulation might also explain the elevated risk when the paternal antigenic load is increased.

156
00:21:00,435 --> 00:21:07,335
One example is complete molar pregnancies, which have diploid complement of chromosomes solely from the father.

157
00:21:07,335 --> 00:21:13,010
Those with later-stage moles, have a high incidence of early-onset preeclampsia.

158
00:21:13,010 --> 00:21:19,810
Women with a trisomy 13 fetus also have a 30- to 40-percent incidence of preeclampsia.

159
00:21:19,810 --> 00:21:27,710
The gene for one preeclampsia-linked factor, soluble fmslike tyrosine kinase 1, is on chromosome 13 .

160
00:21:27,710 --> 00:21:35,960
These women have elevated serum levels of antiangiogenic factors, which can affect the placenta (p. 694).

161
00:21:35,960 --> 00:21:45,360
Last, women previously exposed to paternal antigens, such as a prior pregnancy with the same partner, may be “immunized” against preeclampsia.

162
00:21:45,360 --> 00:21:51,323
Conversely, multiparas impregnated by a new partner have a greater risk of preeclampsia .

163
00:21:51,323 --> 00:21:59,660
Burton and colleagues (2019) reviewed the possible role of immune maladaptation in preeclampsia pathophysiology.

164
00:21:59,660 --> 00:22:11,635
In women destined to have preeclampsia, extravillous trophoblasts early in pregnancy express reduced amounts of immunosuppressive nonclassic human leukocyte antigen G (HLAG).

165
00:22:11,635 --> 00:22:22,535
Black women more commonly have the 1597∆C gene allele, which is associated with incomplete HLA-G expression and may predispose to preeclampsia .

166
00:22:22,535 --> 00:22:29,535
This immune maladaptation may contribute to the defective placental vascularization seen with preeclampsia.

167
00:22:29,535 --> 00:22:41,323
As discussed in Chapter 4 (p. 61), T-helper (Th) lymphocytes during normal pregnancy are produced so that type 2 activity is increased in relation to type 1.

168
00:22:41,323 --> 00:22:48,085
This is the so-called type 2 bias (Phipps, 2019; Redman, 2015).

169
00:22:48,085 --> 00:22:55,973
Th2 cells promote humoral immunity, whereas Th1 cells stimulate inflammatory cytokine secretion .

170
00:22:55,973 --> 00:23:02,973
Beginning in the early second trimester in women who develop preeclampsia, Th1 action is increased.

171
00:23:02,973 --> 00:23:08,985
Genetic Factors Preeclampsia appears to be a multifactorial, polygenic disorder.

172
00:23:08,985 --> 00:23:18,273
In one study of almost 1.2 million Swedish births, a genetic association was found for gestational hypertension and for preeclampsia .

173
00:23:18,273 --> 00:23:34,073
Ward and Taylor (2015) cite an incident risk for preeclampsia of 20 to 40 percent for daughters of preeclamptic mothers; 11 to 37 percent for sisters of preeclamptic women; and 22 to 47 percent for twins.

174
00:23:34,073 --> 00:23:41,260
Ethnoracial factors are important and evidenced by the high incidence of preeclampsia in African American women.

175
00:23:41,260 --> 00:23:47,910
Latina women have a lower incidence because of interactions of American Indian and white race genes .

176
00:23:47,910 --> 00:24:06,085
The hereditary predisposition for preeclampsia likely stems from interactions of literally hundreds of inherited genes—both maternal and paternal—that control many enzymatic and metabolic functions throughout every organ system (Burton, 2019; Triche, 2014).

177
00:24:06,085 --> 00:24:14,785
Plasma-derived factors may induce some of these genes in preeclampsia (Leseva, 2020; Mackenzie, 2012).

178
00:24:14,785 --> 00:24:21,585
Thus, the clinical manifestation in any given woman with the preeclampsia syndrome will reflect a spectrum.

179
00:24:21,585 --> 00:24:24,785
In this regard, phenotypic expression

180
00:24:24,940 --> 00:24:30,940
will differ among similar genotypes depending on interactions with environmental components .

181
00:24:30,890 --> 00:24:42,177
Hundreds of genes have been studied for their possible association with preeclampsia (Buurma, 2013; Sakowicz, 2016; Ward, 2015).

182
00:24:42,177 --> 00:24:51,165
However, because of the complex phenotypic expression of preeclampsia, it is doubtful that any one candidate gene will be found responsible.

183
00:24:51,165 --> 00:25:02,402
Last, a preeclampsia predisposition has also been linked to genes of the fetus (Burton, 2019; Gray, 2018; Leseva, 2020).

184
00:25:02,402 --> 00:25:05,827
# Stage II—Maternal Syndrome.

185
00:25:05,827 --> 00:25:12,765
Endothelial Cell Activation Inflammatory changes are believed to be a continuation of the placental syndrome.

186
00:25:12,765 --> 00:25:24,340
In response to ischemia or other inciting causes, placental factors are released and initiate a series of events (Burton, 2019; Davidge, 2015).

187
00:25:24,340 --> 00:25:38,390
Thus, antiangiogenic and metabolic factors and other inflammatory leukocyte mediators are thought to provoke the systemic endotheliopathy, which is used synonymously here with endothelial cell activation or dysfunction.

188
00:25:38,390 --> 00:25:48,690
Injury to systemic endothelial cells is seen as a centerpiece of preeclampsia pathogenesis (Burton, 2019; Phipps, 2019).

189
00:25:48,690 --> 00:25:55,415
Cellular dysfunction may result from an extreme activated state of leukocytes in the maternal circulation .

190
00:25:55,415 --> 00:26:06,890
Briefly, cytokines such as tumor necrosis factor α (TNF-α) and the interleukins may contribute to the systemic oxidative stress associated with preeclampsia.

191
00:26:06,890 --> 00:26:12,027
This is characterized by generation of highly toxic oxygen radicals.

192
00:26:12,027 --> 00:26:21,465
These injure systemic vascular endothelial cells, lower nitric oxide production by these cells, and interfere with prostaglandin balance.

193
00:26:21,465 --> 00:26:39,815
Other sequelae include production of the lipid-laden macrophage foam cells seen in placental atherosis; activation of systemic microvascular coagulation, which is manifested by thrombocytopenia; and greater systemic capillary permeability, which is reflected by edema and proteinuria.

194
00:26:39,815 --> 00:26:44,077
Intact endothelium has anticoagulant properties.

195
00:26:44,077 --> 00:26:52,765
Also, systemic endothelial cells, by releasing nitric oxide, blunt the response of vascular smooth muscle to agonists.

196
00:26:52,765 --> 00:27:02,590
Injured or activated endothelial cells may produce less nitric oxide and may secrete substances that promote coagulation and vasopressor sensitivity.

197
00:27:02,590 --> 00:27:17,927
Further evidence of endothelial activation includes changes in glomerular capillary endothelial morphology, greater capillary permeability, and elevated blood concentrations of substances associated with endothelial activations.

198
00:27:17,927 --> 00:27:28,302
Likely, multiple factors in the plasma of preeclamptic women combine to exert these vasoactive effects (Myers, 2007; Walsh, 2009).

199
00:27:28,302 --> 00:27:34,215
Vasospasm and Hypertension Vasospasm has long been associated with preeclampsia.

200
00:27:34,215 --> 00:27:41,502
Systemic endothelial activation causes vasospasm, which elevates resistance to produce hypertension.

201
00:27:41,502 --> 00:27:51,465
Concurrently, systemic endothelial cell injury promotes interstitial leakage, and platelets and fibrinogen are deposited in the subendothelial space.

202
00:27:51,465 --> 00:28:04,090
Endothelial junctional proteins are also disrupted, and the subendothelial region of resistance arteries undergoes ultrastructural change (Suzuki, 2003; Wang, 2002).

203
00:28:04,090 --> 00:28:07,877
The much larger venous circuit is similarly involved.

204
00:28:07,877 --> 00:28:20,027
With diminished blood flow because of maldistribution from vasospasm and interstitial leakage, ischemia of the surrounding tissues can cause necrosis, hemorrhage, and other end-organ disturbances.

205
00:28:20,027 --> 00:28:28,052
One important clinical correlate to these changes is the markedly attenuated blood volume seen in women with severe preeclampsia .

206
00:28:28,052 --> 00:28:37,777
Increased Pressor Responses As discussed in Chapter 4 (p. 53), pregnant women normally develop refractoriness to infused vasopressors .

207
00:28:37,777 --> 00:28:50,615
Women with early preeclampsia, however, have enhanced vascular reactivity to infused norepinephrine and angiotensin II (Raab, 1956; Talledo, 1968).

208
00:28:50,615 --> 00:28:58,252
Moreover, increased sensitivity to angiotensin II clearly precedes the onset of gestational hypertension .

209
00:28:58,252 --> 00:29:05,665
Paradoxically, women who develop preterm preeclampsia have lower circulating levels of angiotensin II .

210
00:29:05,665 --> 00:29:11,865
Numerous prostaglandins are thought to be central to preeclampsia syndrome pathophysiology.

211
00:29:11,865 --> 00:29:17,027
Specifically, the blunted pressor response seen in normal pregnancy is at

212
00:29:17,188 --> 00:29:24,375
least partially due to diminished vascular responsiveness mediated by endothelial prostaglandin synthesis.

213
00:29:24,325 --> 00:29:33,375
For example, compared with normal pregnancy, endothelial prostacyclin (PGI2) production is lower in preeclampsia.

214
00:29:33,375 --> 00:29:37,713
This action appears to be mediated by phospholipase A2 .

215
00:29:37,713 --> 00:29:46,813
At the same time, thromboxane A2 secretion by platelets is increased, and the prostacyclin: thromboxane A2 ratio declines.

216
00:29:46,813 --> 00:29:53,225
The net result favors greater sensitivity to infused angiotensin II and vasoconstriction .

217
00:29:53,225 --> 00:29:59,463
These changes appear as early as 22 weeks in gravidas who later develop preeclampsia .

218
00:29:59,463 --> 00:30:06,188
Nitric oxide is a potent vasodilator and is synthesized from 1-arginine by endothelial cells.

219
00:30:06,188 --> 00:30:16,238
Inhibition of nitric oxide synthesis raises mean arterial pressure, lowers heart rate, and reverses the pregnancy-induced refractoriness to vasopressors.

220
00:30:16,238 --> 00:30:25,075
Nitric oxide likely is the compound that maintains the normal low-pressure vasodilated state characteristic of normal fetoplacental perfusion .

221
00:30:25,075 --> 00:30:34,525
Nitric oxide mediates the effects of placental growth factor (PlGF) and vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) in vitro .

222
00:30:34,525 --> 00:30:39,413
The effects of nitric oxide production in preeclampsia are unclear.

223
00:30:39,413 --> 00:30:48,213
It appears that the syndrome is associated with decreased endothelial nitric oxide synthase expression and thus lower nitric oxide activity .

224
00:30:48,213 --> 00:30:53,775
Endothelins are 21-amino-acid peptides and potent vasoconstrictors.

225
00:30:53,775 --> 00:30:59,888
Endothelin l (ET-1) is the primary isoform produced by human endothelium .

226
00:30:59,888 --> 00:31:04,688
Plasma ET-1 levels are elevated in normotensive pregnant women.

227
00:31:04,688 --> 00:31:11,125
Women with preeclampsia have even higher levels, and these peptides may mediate renal injury .

228
00:31:11,125 --> 00:31:18,400
Woman with preeclampsia develop functional autoantibodies to the endothelin and angiotensin II receptors .

229
00:31:18,400 --> 00:31:25,650
Interestingly, treatment of preeclamptic women with magnesium sulfate lowers ET-1 concentrations .

230
00:31:25,650 --> 00:31:30,863
In animal studies, sildenafil reduces ET-1 concentrations .

231
00:31:30,863 --> 00:31:38,763
Angiogenic and Antiangiogenic Factors Placental vasculogenesis is evident by 21 days after conception.

232
00:31:38,763 --> 00:31:49,363
The list of pro- and antiangiogenic substances involved in placental vascular development is extensive, and the VEGF and angiopoietin families are the most studied.

233
00:31:49,363 --> 00:31:59,963
Angiogenic imbalance describes excessive amounts of antiangiogenic factors, which are thought to be stimulated by worsening hypoxia at the uteroplacental interface.

234
00:31:59,963 --> 00:32:08,900
Trophoblast of women destined to develop preeclampsia overproduce at least two antiangiogenic peptides that enter the maternal circulation .

235
00:32:08,900 --> 00:32:18,563
First, soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase 1 (sF1t-1) is a soluble variant of the membrane-bound receptor for VEGF.

236
00:32:18,563 --> 00:32:30,963
As depicted in Figure 40-4, elevated maternal sFlt-1 levels inactivate and reduce circulating PlGF and VEGF concentrations, leading to endothelial dysfunction .

237
00:32:30,963 --> 00:32:41,363
As shown in data from Myatt and coworkers (2013), sFlt-1 levels begin to rise in maternal serum months before preeclampsia is evident .

238
00:32:41,363 --> 00:32:52,075
These high levels in the second trimester are associated with a much higher risk for preeclampsia development (Haggerty, 2012; March, 2015).

239
00:32:52,075 --> 00:32:57,425
This elevation from normal levels appears even sooner with early-onset disease .

240
00:32:57,425 --> 00:33:03,163
These factors are also operative in pregnancies complicated by fetalgrowth restriction .

241
00:33:03,163 --> 00:33:05,488
Figure 40-4.

242
00:33:05,488 --> 00:33:14,950
Schematic of receptor blocking action of soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase 1 (sFlt-1) and soluble endoglin (sENG).

243
00:33:14,950 --> 00:33:17,413
Figure 40-5.

244
00:33:17,413 --> 00:33:25,963
Angiogenic and antiangiogenic factors in normotensive (NT) and preeclamptic (PE) women across pregnancy.

245
00:33:25,963 --> 00:33:32,263
Both pairs of factors are significantly divergent by 23 to 26 weeks’ gestation.

246
00:33:32,263 --> 00:33:40,388
sFlt-1 = soluble fms-like tyrosine kinase 1; PlGF = placental growth factor.

247
00:33:40,388 --> 00:33:54,488
A second antiangiogenic peptide, soluble endoglin (sEng), inhibits various transforming growth factor beta (TGF-β) isoforms from binding to endothelial receptors (see Fig. 40-4).

248
00:33:54,488 --> 00:33:57,288
Endoglin is one of these receptors.

249
00:33:57,288 --> 00:34:03,625
Decreased binding to endoglin diminishes endothelial nitric oxide-dependent vasodilation.

250
00:34:03,625 --> 00:34:09,650
Serum levels of sEng also begin to rise months before clinical preeclampsia develops .

251
00:34:09,650 --> 00:34:17,388
Simultaneously elevated levels of sFlt-1 and sEng are associated with more severe forms of preeclampsia .

252
00:34:17,388 --> 00:34:19,600
In one systematic

253
00:34:19,756 --> 00:34:31,543
review, third-trimester increases in sFlt-1 levels and lower PlGF concentrations were found to correlate with preeclampsia development after 25 weeks’ gestation .

254
00:34:31,493 --> 00:34:41,256
From another study, doubling of sFlt-1 and sEng expression increased the preeclampsia risk by 39 and 74 percent, respectively .

255
00:34:41,256 --> 00:34:47,031
The cause of placental overproduction of antiangiogenic proteins remains an enigma.

256
00:34:47,031 --> 00:34:57,793
Concentrations of the soluble forms are not higher in fetal circulation or amnionic fluid of preeclamptic women, and their levels in maternal blood dissipate after delivery .

257
00:34:57,793 --> 00:35:06,793
Clinical use of antiangiogenic protein levels to predict and diagnosis preeclampsia is being evaluated (p. 703).

258
00:35:06,793 --> 00:35:13,943
Moreover, one preliminary report described therapeutic apheresis to reduce sFlt-1 levels .

259
00:35:13,943 --> 00:35:16,181
Pathophysiology.

260
00:35:16,181 --> 00:35:19,181
# Cardiovascular System.

261
00:35:19,181 --> 00:35:24,581
Disturbances in the cardiovascular system are common with preeclampsia syndrome.

262
00:35:24,581 --> 00:35:48,868
These are related to: (1) greater cardiac afterload imposed by hypertension; (2) cardiac preload, which is reduced by a pathologically diminished volume expansion during pregnancy and which is increased by administration of intravenous crystalloid or oncotic solutions; and (3) endothelial activation leading to leakage of intravascular fluid into the extracellular space.

263
00:35:48,868 --> 00:35:57,143
Hemodynamic Changes and Cardiac Function The cardiovascular aberrations of pregnancy-related hypertensive disorders vary.

264
00:35:57,143 --> 00:36:08,381
Modifying factors include preeclampsia severity, degree of hypertension, presence of underlying chronic disease, and the point in the clinical spectrum in which these are studied.

265
00:36:08,381 --> 00:36:19,368
In some women, these cardiovascular changes may precede hypertension (Easterling, 1990; Khalil, 2012; Melchiorre, 2013).

266
00:36:19,368 --> 00:36:28,356
Nevertheless, with the clinical onset of preeclampsia, cardiac output declines, due at least in part to greater peripheral resistance .

267
00:36:28,356 --> 00:36:41,393
Myocardial Function Serial echocardiographic studies document diastolic dysfunction in up to 45 percent of women with preeclampsia (Guirguis, 2015; Vaught, 2018).

268
00:36:41,393 --> 00:36:47,393
With this dysfunction, ventricles do not properly relax and cannot fill appropriately.

269
00:36:47,393 --> 00:36:57,218
In some affected women, functional differences persist up to 4 years after delivery (Evans, 2011; Orabona, 2017).

270
00:36:57,218 --> 00:37:08,093
Diastolic dysfunction stems from ventricular remodeling, which is a maladaptive response to the increased afterload of preeclampsia and aims to maintain normal contractility.

271
00:37:08,093 --> 00:37:12,693
High levels of antiangiogenic proteins may be contributory .

272
00:37:12,693 --> 00:37:18,043
In otherwise healthy gravidas, these changes are usually inconsequential.

273
00:37:18,043 --> 00:37:31,006
But in those with underlying ventricular dysfunction—for example, concentric ventricular hypertrophy from chronic hypertension— further diastolic dysfunction may cause cardiogenic pulmonary edema .

274
00:37:31,006 --> 00:37:38,081
This is discussed further in Chapters 50 (p. 883) and 52 (p. 916).

275
00:37:38,081 --> 00:37:48,731
Ventricular Function Despite the relatively high frequency of diastolic dysfunction with preeclampsia, clinical cardiac function in most affected women is appropriate .

276
00:37:48,731 --> 00:37:55,081
In some preeclamptic women, high-sensitivity cardiac troponin levels are slightly elevated .

277
00:37:55,081 --> 00:38:03,331
With severe preeclampsia, amino-terminal pro–brain natriuretic peptide (NT–pro-BNP) levels are increased .

278
00:38:03,331 --> 00:38:09,256
Women with preeclampsia syndrome usually have slightly hyperdynamic ventricular function .

279
00:38:09,256 --> 00:38:16,493
Both these and normotensive pregnant women have a cardiac output that is appropriate for left-sided filling pressures.

280
00:38:16,493 --> 00:38:20,631
This pressure can be altered by intravenous fluid volumes.

281
00:38:20,631 --> 00:38:26,481
Thus, aggressive hydration results in overtly hyperdynamic ventricular function.

282
00:38:26,481 --> 00:38:35,431
This is accompanied by elevated pulmonary capillary wedge pressures, and pulmonary edema may develop despite normal ventricular function.

283
00:38:35,431 --> 00:38:45,206
This is partly because of an alveolar endothelial-epithelial leak, and it is compounded by decreased oncotic pressure from a low serum albumin concentration.

284
00:38:45,206 --> 00:38:59,118
In sum, aggressive fluid administration to otherwise normal women with severe preeclampsia substantially elevates normal left-sided filling pressures and raises a physiologically normal cardiac output to hyperdynamic levels.

285
00:38:59,118 --> 00:39:01,543
Figure 40-6.

286
00:39:01,543 --> 00:39:07,368
Ventricular function in normally pregnant women (striped area) and in women with

287
00:39:07,540 --> 00:39:13,215
eclampsia (boxed area) is plotted on a Braunwald ventricular function curve.

288
00:39:13,165 --> 00:39:20,465
Normal values are from Clark, 1989, and those for eclampsia are from Hankins, 1984.

289
00:39:20,465 --> 00:39:29,090
PCWP = pulmonary capillary wedge pressure; LVSWI = left ventricular stroke work index.

290
00:39:29,090 --> 00:39:31,427
# Blood Volume.

291
00:39:31,427 --> 00:39:36,202
In those with eclampsia, hemoconcentration is a hallmark feature .

292
00:39:36,202 --> 00:39:44,977
Data from Zeeman and associates (2009) show that normally expected pregnancy blood volume expansion is severely curtailed .

293
00:39:44,977 --> 00:39:56,702
Women of average size have a nonpregnant blood volume of 3000 mL, and during the last several weeks of a normal pregnancy, this averages 4500 mL .

294
00:39:56,702 --> 00:40:04,090
With eclampsia, however, much or all of the anticipated 1500 mL excess is lost.

295
00:40:04,090 --> 00:40:13,902
Such hemoconcentration results from generalized vasospasm that follows endothelial activation and then from leakage of plasma into the interstitial space.

296
00:40:13,902 --> 00:40:20,815
In women with preeclampsia—depending on its severity— hemoconcentration is usually not as marked.

297
00:40:20,815 --> 00:40:23,202
Figure 40-7.

298
00:40:23,202 --> 00:40:28,252
Total blood volumes in normotensive women compared with those with eclampsia.

299
00:40:28,252 --> 00:40:32,640
The vertical extensions are one standard deviation from the mean.

300
00:40:32,640 --> 00:40:39,840
In the eclamptic women, note the minimally increased blood volume compared with a subsequent normotensive pregnancy.

301
00:40:39,840 --> 00:40:43,915
These changes have substantial clinical consequences.

302
00:40:43,915 --> 00:40:51,665
Women with severe hemoconcentration are unduly sensitive to blood loss at delivery that otherwise may be considered normal.

303
00:40:51,665 --> 00:41:00,040
Vasospasm and endothelial leakage of plasma persist for a variable time after delivery as the endothelium is restored to normal.

304
00:41:00,040 --> 00:41:08,765
As this takes place, vasoconstriction reverses, and as the blood volume reexpands, the hematocrit usually falls from dilution.

305
00:41:08,765 --> 00:41:15,590
Importantly, a substantive cause of this fall in hematocrit frequently is the blood loss incurred at delivery.

306
00:41:15,590 --> 00:41:18,552
# Hematological Changes.

307
00:41:18,552 --> 00:41:25,152
Thrombocytopenia The platelet count is routinely measured in women with any form of gestational hypertension.

308
00:41:25,152 --> 00:41:36,602
The frequency and intensity of thrombocytopenia vary and are dependent on the severity and duration of preeclampsia (Hellmann, 2007; Hupuczi, 2007).

309
00:41:36,602 --> 00:41:44,765
Overt thrombocytopenia—defined by a platelet count <100,000/µL—reflects severe disease .

310
00:41:44,765 --> 00:41:51,927
In general, the lower the platelet count, the higher the likelihood of maternal and fetal morbidity and mortality.

311
00:41:51,927 --> 00:41:57,815
In most cases, delivery is advisable because thrombocytopenia usually worsens.

312
00:41:57,815 --> 00:42:03,352
After delivery, the platelet count may continue to decline for the first day or so.

313
00:42:03,352 --> 00:42:08,965
It then usually rises progressively to reach a normal level within 3 to 5 days.

314
00:42:08,965 --> 00:42:16,965
As discussed later, in some instances with HELLP syndrome, the platelet count continues to fall after delivery.

315
00:42:16,965 --> 00:42:27,277
If this nadir is delayed until 48 to 72 hours, preeclampsia syndrome may be incorrectly attributed to one of the thrombotic microangiopathies .

316
00:42:27,277 --> 00:42:32,702
Another platelet alteration is platelet activation and increased αdegranulation.

317
00:42:32,702 --> 00:42:38,865
This leads to release of β-thromboglobulin and factor 4 and to enhanced platelet clearance .

318
00:42:38,865 --> 00:42:43,640
Platelet volume concomitantly increases as young platelets are released .

319
00:42:43,640 --> 00:42:52,577
Paradoxically, in most studies, in vitro platelet aggregation is reduced compared with the normal increase that is characteristic of pregnancy.

320
00:42:52,577 --> 00:42:57,602
This likely is due to platelet “exhaustion” following in-vivo activation.

321
00:42:57,602 --> 00:43:06,365
Although the cause is unknown, immunological processes or simply platelet deposition at sites of endothelial damage may be implicated.

322
00:43:06,365 --> 00:43:14,827
Levels of platelet-bound and circulating platelet-bindable immunoglobulins are elevated, which suggests platelet surface alterations.

323
00:43:14,827 --> 00:43:26,790
Abnormally low platelet levels do not develop in the fetuses of women with preeclampsia despite severe maternal thrombocytopenia (Kenny, 2015; Pritchard, 1987).

324
00:43:26,790 --> 00:43:33,615
Thus, thrombocytopenia in a hypertensive woman is not a fetal indication for cesarean delivery.

325
00:43:33,615 --> 00:43:44,840
Hemolysis Severe preeclampsia is frequently accompanied by hemolysis, which manifests as elevated serum lactate dehydrogenase levels and reduced haptoglobin levels .

326
00:43:44,840 --> 00:43:57,765
Other evidence comes from schizocytosis, spherocytosis, and reticulocytosis in peripheral blood (Cunningham, 1985; Pritchard, 1954, 1976).

327
00:43:57,765 --> 00:44:01,352
Red cell distribution width (RDW)

328
00:44:01,516 --> 00:44:09,391
reflects variability in the size of circulating red blood cells, and RDW is higher in preeclamptic women .

329
00:44:09,341 --> 00:44:18,766
These derangements result in part from microangiopathic hemolysis caused by endothelial disruption with platelet adherence and fibrin deposition.

330
00:44:18,766 --> 00:44:28,566
Cunningham and coworkers (1995) postulated that erythrocyte morphological changes were partially caused by serum lipid alterations.

331
00:44:28,566 --> 00:44:36,466
Related, substantively decreased long-chain fatty acid content is found in erythrocytes of women with preeclampsia .

332
00:44:36,466 --> 00:44:49,378
After early reports of hemolysis and thrombocytopenia with severe preeclampsia, descriptions were added of abnormally elevated serum liver transaminase levels that indicated hepatocellular necrosis .

333
00:44:49,378 --> 00:44:57,778
Weinstein (1982) referred to this combination of events as the HELLP syndrome (p. 699).

334
00:44:57,778 --> 00:45:10,966
Coagulation Changes Subtle changes consistent with intravascular coagulation commonly are found with preeclampsia and eclampsia (Cunningham, 2015; von Dadelszen, 2018).

335
00:45:10,966 --> 00:45:24,853
Some include elevated factor VIII consumption, increased levels of fibrinopeptides A and B and of D-dimers, and reduced concentrations of the regulatory proteins—antithrombin III and proteins C and S.

336
00:45:24,853 --> 00:45:34,178
Coagulation aberrations generally are mild and seldom clinically significant (Kenny, 2015; Pritchard, 1984).

337
00:45:34,178 --> 00:45:42,703
Unless placental abruption is comorbid, plasma fibrinogen levels do not differ remarkably from levels found in normal pregnancy .

338
00:45:42,703 --> 00:45:52,203
As preeclampsia worsens, so do abnormal findings with thromboelastography, which is described in Chapter 44 (p. 774) .

339
00:45:52,203 --> 00:46:09,716
Despite these changes, routine laboratory assessments of coagulation, such as prothrombin time (PT), activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT), and plasma fibrinogen level, are not required in the management of pregnancy-associated hypertensive disorders.

340
00:46:09,716 --> 00:46:13,278
# Endocrine and Hormonal Alterations.

341
00:46:13,278 --> 00:46:25,841
Plasma levels of renin, angiotensin II, aldosterone, deoxycorticosterone, and atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) are substantively augmented during normal pregnancy.

342
00:46:25,841 --> 00:46:33,878
ANP is released during atrial wall stretching from blood volume expansion, and it responds to cardiac contractility .

343
00:46:33,878 --> 00:46:40,703
Serum ANP levels rise in pregnancy, and its secretion is further enhanced in preeclampsia .

344
00:46:40,703 --> 00:46:47,741
Levels of its precursor—proatrial natriuretic peptide—also are elevated in preeclampsia.

345
00:46:47,741 --> 00:46:59,453
Vasopressin levels are similar in nonpregnant, in normally pregnant, and in preeclamptic women, although its metabolic clearance is elevated in the latter two (Dürr, 1999).

346
00:46:59,453 --> 00:47:03,078
# Fluid and Electrolyte Alterations.

347
00:47:03,078 --> 00:47:13,278
In women with severe preeclampsia, the volume of extracellular fluid, which manifests as edema, is usually much greater than that in normal pregnant women.

348
00:47:13,278 --> 00:47:22,341
The mechanism responsible for pathological fluid retention is endothelial injury and subsequent extravasation of intravascular fluid.

349
00:47:22,341 --> 00:47:26,578
Affected women also have reduced plasma oncotic pressure.

350
00:47:26,578 --> 00:47:31,791
This further displaces intravascular fluid into the surrounding interstitium.

351
00:47:31,791 --> 00:47:39,491
In women with preeclampsia, electrolyte concentrations do not differ appreciably from those of normal pregnant women.

352
00:47:39,491 --> 00:47:50,441
Following an eclamptic convulsion, the serum pH and bicarbonate concentration are lowered due to lactic acidosis and compensatory respiratory loss of carbon dioxide.

353
00:47:50,441 --> 00:48:01,803
The intensity of acidosis relates to the amount of lactic acid produced— metabolic acidosis—and the rate at which carbon dioxide is exhaled—respiratory acidosis.

354
00:48:01,803 --> 00:48:03,816
# Kidney.

355
00:48:03,816 --> 00:48:10,928
During normal pregnancy, renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate (GFR) rise appreciably .

356
00:48:10,928 --> 00:48:16,178
With preeclampsia, several reversible physiological changes ensue.

357
00:48:16,178 --> 00:48:21,516
Of clinical importance, renal perfusion and GFR are slightly reduced.

358
00:48:21,516 --> 00:48:33,716
Most of the decrement in GFR is from increased renal afferent arteriolar resistance that may be elevated up to fivefold (Conrad, 2015; Cornelis, 2011).

359
00:48:33,716 --> 00:48:40,491
Morphological changes are characterized by glomerular endotheliosis, which blocks filtration .

360
00:48:40,491 --> 00:48:51,241
Diminished filtration causes serum creatinine levels to rise to values seen in nonpregnant individuals, that is, 1 mg/mL, and sometimes higher.

361
00:48:51,241 --> 00:48:54,953
Acute kidney injury is discussed subsequently.

362
00:48:54,953 --> 00:48:58,266
Plasma uric acid concentration is

363
00:48:58,420 --> 00:49:01,507
typically elevated in preeclampsia.

364
00:49:01,457 --> 00:49:09,245
The elevation exceeds that attributable to the reduced GFR and likely is also due to enhanced tubular reabsorption .

365
00:49:09,245 --> 00:49:18,657
At the same time, preeclampsia is associated with diminished urinary excretion of calcium, perhaps because of greater tubular reabsorption .

366
00:49:18,657 --> 00:49:26,195
Proteinuria Detection of proteinuria helps to establish the diagnosis of preeclampsia (see Table 40-1.

367
00:49:26,195 --> 00:49:46,332
Abnormal protein excretion is empirically defined by 24-hour urinary excretion exceeding 300 mg; a spot urine protein: creatinine ratio ≥0.3; or persistent protein values of 30 mg/dL (1+ dipstick) in random urine samples.

368
00:49:46,332 --> 00:50:01,707
Although worsening or nephrotic-range proteinuria was in the past considered by most to be a sign of severe disease, this does not appear to be the case (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2013; Bartal, 2020).

369
00:50:01,707 --> 00:50:09,732
Problematically, the optimal method of establishing abnormal levels of either urine protein or albumin remains to be defined.

370
00:50:09,732 --> 00:50:25,470
For a 24-hour quantitative specimen, the consensus threshold value is ≥300 mg/24 h (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2013; Bartal, 2020).

371
00:50:25,470 --> 00:50:34,070
Using a urinary protein excretion threshold of 165 mg in a 12-hour sample shows equivalent efficacy .

372
00:50:34,070 --> 00:50:41,670
Determination of urinary protein: creatinine ratio may supplant the cumbersome 24-hour quantification .

373
00:50:41,670 --> 00:50:58,832
In one systematic review, random urine protein:creatinine ratios <130 to 150 mg/g, that is, 0.13 to 0.15, indicate a low likelihood of proteinuria exceeding 300 mg/d .

374
00:50:58,832 --> 00:51:09,520
Ratios <0.08 or >1.19 have negative and positive predictive values of 86 and 96 percent, respectively .

375
00:51:09,520 --> 00:51:18,857
However, midrange ratios, for example, 300 mg/g or 0.3, have poor sensitivity and specificity.

376
00:51:18,857 --> 00:51:27,907
Any midrange ratio should be repeated, and if persistent, a 24hour urine collection for measurement of protein excretion should be considered.

377
00:51:27,907 --> 00:51:36,045
With urine dipstick assessment, results depend on urine concentration and are notorious for false-positive and -negative results.

378
00:51:36,045 --> 00:51:53,282
A concentrated urine specimen may show a dipstick value of 1+ to 2+ in women who actually excrete <300 mg/d. Importantly, proteinuria may develop late, and some women may already be delivered or have had an eclamptic convulsion before it appears.

379
00:51:53,282 --> 00:52:00,132
At presentation, 10 to 15 percent of women with HELLP syndrome do not have proteinuria .

380
00:52:00,132 --> 00:52:07,007
In another report, 17 percent of women with eclampsia did not have proteinuria by the time of seizures .

381
00:52:07,007 --> 00:52:17,407
Anatomical Changes Sheehan and Lynch (1973) frequently found microscopic changes that were identified at autopsy in the kidneys of eclamptic women.

382
00:52:17,407 --> 00:52:26,207
Glomeruli are enlarged by approximately 20 percent, they are “bloodless,” and capillary loops variably are dilated and contracted.

383
00:52:26,207 --> 00:52:31,895
Endothelial cells are swollen— termed glomerular capillary endotheliosis .

384
00:52:31,895 --> 00:52:37,345
Such swelling may be severe enough to block or partially block the capillary lumens .

385
00:52:37,345 --> 00:52:43,895
Last, homogeneous subendothelial deposits of proteins and fibrin-like material are seen .

386
00:52:43,895 --> 00:52:46,232
Figure 40-8.

387
00:52:46,232 --> 00:52:50,532
Schematic showing glomerular capillary endotheliosis.

388
00:52:50,532 --> 00:53:00,907
The capillary of the normal glomerulus shown on the left has wide endothelial fenestrations, and the pedicels emanating from the podocytes are widely spaced (arrow).

389
00:53:00,907 --> 00:53:07,220
The illustration on the right is of a glomerulus with changes induced by the preeclampsia syndrome.

390
00:53:07,220 --> 00:53:13,882
The endothelial cells are swollen and their fenestrae narrowed, as are the pedicels that now abut each other.

391
00:53:13,882 --> 00:53:18,745
Endothelial swelling may result from angiogenic protein “withdrawal.

392
00:53:18,745 --> 00:53:28,895
This is caused by the complexing of free angiogenic proteins with a compatible circulating antiangiogenic protein receptor (see Fig. 40-4).

393
00:53:28,895 --> 00:53:41,595
The angiogenic proteins are crucial for podocyte health, and their inactivation leads to podocyte dysfunction and endothelial swelling (Conrad, 2015; Phipps, 2019).

394
00:53:41,595 --> 00:53:47,095
Eclampsia is characterized by greater excretion of these epithelial podocytes .

395
00:53:47,095 --> 00:53:59,257
Acute Kidney Injury In one study, preeclampsia syndrome caused acute kidney injury (AKI) in 5 percent of patients and in 14 percent of those with HELLP syndrome .

396
00:53:59,257 --> 00:54:03,532
With severe preeclampsia, Rodriguez and colleagues

397
00:54:03,700 --> 00:54:10,275
2021) reported AKI in 15 percent of women in a study from Parkland Hospital.

398
00:54:10,225 --> 00:54:13,362
In most, AKI was stage 1.

399
00:54:13,362 --> 00:54:22,625
In another study of 72 women with preeclampsia and renal failure, half had HELLP syndrome, and a third had placental abruption .

400
00:54:22,625 --> 00:54:29,562
In a review of 183 women with HELLP syndrome, 5 percent had AKI .

401
00:54:29,562 --> 00:54:35,737
Of those with renal injury, half had placental abruption, and most had postpartum hemorrhage.

402
00:54:35,737 --> 00:54:45,475
Abnormal renal values usually begin to normalize 10 days or later after delivery (Cornelis, 2011; Spaan, 2012).

403
00:54:45,475 --> 00:54:57,412
Although mild degrees of AKI are encountered, clinically apparent acute tubular necrosis is almost invariably induced by comorbid hemorrhage and subsequent hypovolemia and hypotension .

404
00:54:57,412 --> 00:55:03,387
To evaluate AKI etiology clinically, urine electrolytes may be obtained.

405
00:55:03,387 --> 00:55:07,537
Results with preeclampsia reflect an intrarenal cause.

406
00:55:07,537 --> 00:55:12,912
In most with preeclampsia, the urine sodium concentration is elevated.

407
00:55:12,912 --> 00:55:24,700
Instead, changes that indicate a prerenal mechanism include increase urine osmolality, elevated urine: plasma creatinine ratio, and low fractional excretion of sodium.

408
00:55:24,700 --> 00:55:31,250
In response to oliguria, sodium-containing crystalloid temporarily improves urine output.

409
00:55:31,250 --> 00:55:38,350
However, rapid infusions may cause clinically apparent pulmonary edema (p. 695).

410
00:55:38,350 --> 00:55:50,437
Intensive intravenous fluid therapy is not indicated as “treatment” for women with preeclampsia and oliguria, unless urine output is diminished from hemorrhage or fluid loss from vomiting or fever.

411
00:55:50,437 --> 00:55:57,975
In nonpregnant individuals, intravenous saline infusions have demonstrated a negative impact on renal function.

412
00:55:57,975 --> 00:56:07,662
However, at Parkland Hospital, transition from Ringer lactate to normal saline did not significantly impair renal function in women with preeclampsia .

413
00:56:07,662 --> 00:56:09,612
# Liver.

414
00:56:09,612 --> 00:56:14,350
Hepatic changes are common in women with severe preeclampsia syndrome.

415
00:56:14,350 --> 00:56:21,700
Several gross and microscopic anatomical derangements lead to elevated serum hepatic transaminase levels.

416
00:56:21,700 --> 00:56:28,225
This transaminitis indicates hepatocellular injury and is a marker for severe preeclampsia.

417
00:56:28,225 --> 00:56:35,600
Values are seldom more than 500 U/L, but levels exceeding 2000 U/L have been reported .

418
00:56:35,600 --> 00:56:45,050
In general, serum concentrations inversely follow platelet levels, and they both usually return to normal levels within 3 days after delivery.

419
00:56:45,050 --> 00:56:56,512
Anatomical Changes Regions of periportal hemorrhage in the liver periphery typify the hepatic lesions of eclampsia (Hecht, 2017; Sheehan, 1973).

420
00:56:56,512 --> 00:57:01,462
Extensive involvement such as shown in Figure 40-9 is unusual.

421
00:57:01,462 --> 00:57:11,112
Sheehan and Lynch (1973) described that some degree of hepatic infarction accompanied hemorrhage in almost half of women who died with eclampsia.

422
00:57:11,112 --> 00:57:19,225
These findings corresponded with reports during the 1960s that described elevated serum hepatic transaminase levels.

423
00:57:19,225 --> 00:57:26,287
Pritchard and associates (1954) described hemolysis and thrombocytopenia with eclampsia.

424
00:57:26,287 --> 00:57:34,787
This constellation of hemolysis, hepatocellular necrosis, and thrombocytopenia was later termed HELLP syndrome.

425
00:57:34,787 --> 00:57:41,962
Similarities with hepatic sinusoidal obstruction are reviewed by von Salmuth and coworkers (2020).

426
00:57:41,962 --> 00:57:44,350
Figure 40-9.

427
00:57:44,350 --> 00:57:50,250
Hepatic section from a woman with HELLP syndrome who died of aspiration pneumonia.

428
00:57:50,250 --> 00:57:57,687
There are areas of ischemia and infarction, and histopathological examination showed periportal hemorrhages.

429
00:57:57,687 --> 00:58:03,225
Liver involvement with preeclampsia may clinically display several manifestations.

430
00:58:03,225 --> 00:58:07,162
First, pain is considered a sign of severe disease.

431
00:58:07,162 --> 00:58:13,875
It typically manifests as moderate to severe right upper quadrant or midepigastric pain and tenderness.

432
00:58:13,875 --> 00:58:22,362
These women usually have elevated serum aspartate transaminase (AST) or alanine transaminase (ALT) levels.

433
00:58:22,362 --> 00:58:31,550
In some cases, however, the amount of hepatic tissue involved with infarction may be surprisingly extensive yet still clinically insignificant.

434
00:58:31,550 --> 00:58:39,187
To study this, we performed magnetic resonance (MR) imaging in 16 women with HELLP syndrome .

435
00:58:39,187 --> 00:58:46,787
All but two had evidence of acute liver injury, and the volume of involvement correlated with serum AST levels.

436
00:58:46,787 --> 00:58:55,637
Frank infarction is unusual, and in our experiences, it may be worsened or precipitated by hypotension from obstetrical hemorrhage.

437
00:58:55,637 --> 00:59:02,225
It occasionally causes hepatic failure—also called shock liver (Morgan, 2019;

438
00:59:02,380 --> 00:59:05,417
Yoshihara, 2016).

439
00:59:05,367 --> 00:59:13,692
Hepatic Hematoma In another presentation, periportal hemorrhage and infarction may extend to develop a hepatic hematoma.

440
00:59:13,692 --> 00:59:18,842
This in turn can extend to form a subcapsular hematoma that may rupture.

441
00:59:18,842 --> 00:59:25,067
Computed tomography (CT) scanning or MR imaging greatly aids diagnosis .

442
00:59:25,067 --> 00:59:33,292
Unruptured hematomas are probably more common than clinically suspected and are more likely to be found with HELLP syndrome .

443
00:59:33,292 --> 00:59:42,080
Although once considered a surgical condition, current management of a hepatic hematoma is usually observation unless bleeding is ongoing.

444
00:59:42,080 --> 00:59:49,267
In some cases, however, prompt surgical intervention or angiographic embolization may be lifesaving .

445
00:59:49,267 --> 01:00:01,780
In one review of 180 cases of hepatic hematoma or rupture, 94 percent of affected gravidas had HELLP syndrome, and in 90 percent of the total, the capsule had ruptured .

446
01:00:01,780 --> 01:00:08,680
The maternal mortality rate was 22 percent, and the perinatal mortality rate was 31 percent.

447
01:00:08,680 --> 01:00:13,080
Another review of 73 cases found similar outcomes .

448
01:00:13,080 --> 01:00:17,292
In rare cases, liver transplantation is necessary .

449
01:00:17,292 --> 01:00:19,605
Figure 40-10.

450
01:00:19,605 --> 01:00:27,255
Abdominal CT imaging performed postpartum in a woman with severe HELLP syndrome and right-upper quadrant pain.

451
01:00:27,255 --> 01:00:35,492
A large subcapsular hematoma (asterisk) is seen confluent with intrahepatic infarction and hematoma (arrowhead).

452
01:00:35,492 --> 01:00:40,792
Numerous flame-shaped hemorrhages are seen at the hematoma interface (arrows).

453
01:00:40,792 --> 01:00:49,442
Acute fatty liver of pregnancy is sometimes confused with preeclampsia (Byrne, 2020; Nelson, 2013).

454
01:00:49,442 --> 01:00:59,905
It too has an onset in late pregnancy, and often hypertension, elevated serum transaminase and creatinine levels, and thrombocytopenia are comorbid.

455
01:00:59,905 --> 01:01:05,155
In distinction, the hallmark of acute fatty liver is marked liver dysfunction.

456
01:01:05,155 --> 01:01:10,030
Liver function overall is usually normal in HELLP syndrome.

457
01:01:10,030 --> 01:01:16,117
Table 58-1 (p. 1031) highlights these clinical differences.

458
01:01:16,117 --> 01:01:21,317
No convincing data link pancreatic involvement with preeclampsia syndrome.

459
01:01:21,317 --> 01:01:27,067
In 407 women with severe preeclampsia, the incidence was 1 percent .

460
01:01:27,067 --> 01:01:33,305
That said, the occasional case of concurrent hemorrhagic pancreatitis is likely unrelated .

461
01:01:33,305 --> 01:01:41,367
In our experiences from Parkland Hospital, lipase and amylase levels are seldom elevated in women with preeclampsia .

462
01:01:41,367 --> 01:01:49,080
HELLP Syndrome This acronym stands for hemolysis, elevated liver enzyme levels, and low platelet count.

463
01:01:49,080 --> 01:01:55,805
No strict definition of the syndrome is universally accepted, and thus its reported incidence varies.

464
01:01:55,805 --> 01:02:06,392
In women with preeclampsia, those with HELLP syndrome typically have worse outcomes than those without it (Martin, 2012, 2013).

465
01:02:06,392 --> 01:02:16,005
In the previously noted study of 183 women with HELLP syndrome, 40 percent had adverse outcomes, and two mothers died .

466
01:02:16,005 --> 01:02:26,267
Complications included eclampsia in 6 percent, placental abruption—10 percent, AKI—5 percent, and pulmonary edema—10 percent.

467
01:02:26,267 --> 01:02:34,580
Stroke, hepatic hematoma, coagulopathy, acute respiratory distress syndrome, and sepsis were other complications.

468
01:02:34,580 --> 01:02:42,105
In one review of 693 women with HELLP syndrome, 10 percent had concurrent eclampsia .

469
01:02:42,105 --> 01:02:45,355
Obstetrical outcomes also may suffer.

470
01:02:45,355 --> 01:03:03,755
In one study comparing women with HELLP against those with preeclampsia, rates of eclampsia were greater with HELLP—15 versus 4 percent; preterm birth— 93 versus 78 percent; and perinatal mortality—9 versus 4 percent, respectively .

471
01:03:03,755 --> 01:03:14,967
Because of these marked clinical differences, some postulate that HELLP syndrome has a distinct pathogenesis (Reimer, 2013; Vaught, 2016).

472
01:03:14,967 --> 01:03:17,692
# Central Nervous System.

473
01:03:17,692 --> 01:03:25,142
Headaches and visual symptoms are common with severe preeclampsia, and associated convulsions define eclampsia.

474
01:03:25,142 --> 01:03:35,855
The earliest anatomical descriptions of brain involvement came from autopsy specimens, but CT and MR imaging and Doppler studies have added important insights.

475
01:03:35,855 --> 01:03:47,542
Neuroanatomical Lesions From early anatomical descriptions, brain pathology accounted for only approximately a third of fatal cases, such as the one shown in Figure 40-11.

476
01:03:47,542 --> 01:03:53,655
In fact, most deaths were from pulmonary edema, and brain lesions were coincidental.

477
01:03:53,655 --> 01:04:01,780
Thus, although gross intracerebral hemorrhage was seen in up to 60 percent of eclamptic women, it was fatal in only half of these .

478
01:04:01,780 --> 01:04:07,730
With data from Sheehan and Lynch (1973) shown in Figure 40-12,

479
01:04:07,900 --> 01:04:15,362
cortical and subcortical petechial hemorrhages are other principal lesions found at autopsy in women with eclampsia.

480
01:04:15,312 --> 01:04:24,737
The classic microscopic vascular lesions consist of fibrinoid necrosis of the arterial wall and perivascular microinfarcts and hemorrhages.

481
01:04:24,737 --> 01:04:36,900
Other lesions include nonhemorrhagic areas of “softening” throughout the brain, hemorrhages in the white matter, and subcortical edema (Hecht, 2017; Willard, 2018).

482
01:04:36,900 --> 01:04:43,225
Hemorrhage in the basal ganglia or pons, often with rupture into the ventricles, may develop.

483
01:04:43,225 --> 01:04:45,662
Figure 40-11.

484
01:04:45,662 --> 01:04:52,200
This autopsy brain slice shows a fatal hypertensive hemorrhage in a primigravida with eclampsia.

485
01:04:52,200 --> 01:04:54,600
Figure 40-12.

486
01:04:54,600 --> 01:05:01,475
Composite illustration showing location of cerebral hemorrhages and petechiae in women with eclampsia.

487
01:05:01,475 --> 01:05:06,250
Insert shows the level of the brain from which the main image was constructed.

488
01:05:06,250 --> 01:05:17,475
Cerebrovascular Pathophysiology Clinical, pathological, and neuroimaging findings have led to two general theories to explain cerebral abnormalities with eclampsia.

489
01:05:17,475 --> 01:05:21,962
Endothelial cell dysfunction likely plays a key role in both.

490
01:05:21,962 --> 01:05:32,375
The first theory suggests that in response to acute and severe hypertension, cerebrovascular overregulation leads to vasospasm and eventual tissue infarction .

491
01:05:32,375 --> 01:05:35,937
Little objective evidence supports this mechanism.

492
01:05:35,937 --> 01:05:44,200
The second theory is that sudden elevations in systemic blood pressure exceed the normal cerebrovascular autoregulatory capacity .

493
01:05:44,200 --> 01:05:51,237
Regions of forced vasodilation and vasoconstriction develop, especially in arterial boundary zones.

494
01:05:51,237 --> 01:06:04,025
At the capillary level, disruption of end-capillary pressure causes increased hydrostatic pressure, hyperperfusion, and extravasation of plasma and red cells through endothelial tight-junction openings.

495
01:06:04,025 --> 01:06:06,962
This leads to vasogenic edema.

496
01:06:06,962 --> 01:06:10,900
Most likely, the true mechanism combines these two.

497
01:06:10,900 --> 01:06:28,162
Thus, a preeclampsia-associated interendothelial cell leak develops at blood pressure levels much lower than those that usually cause vasogenic edema, and this is coupled with a loss of upper-limit autoregulation (Fugate, 2015; Zeeman, 2009).

498
01:06:28,162 --> 01:06:37,050
As shown in Figure 40-13, these abnormalities manifest as the posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES).

499
01:06:37,050 --> 01:06:49,650
Lesions principally involve the occipital and parietal cortices, but other areas are often involved, although less extensively (Edlow, 2013; Zeeman, 2004a).

500
01:06:49,650 --> 01:06:52,287
Figure 40-13.

501
01:06:52,287 --> 01:06:56,950
Cranial magnetic resonance imaging in a nullipara with eclampsia.

502
01:06:56,950 --> 01:07:05,050
This T2-FLAIR image shows an occipital lesion consistent with posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome (PRES).

503
01:07:05,050 --> 01:07:08,912
FLAIR = fluid-attenuated inversion recovery.

504
01:07:08,912 --> 01:07:19,062
Cerebral Blood Flow Autoregulation is the mechanism by which cerebral blood flow remains relatively constant despite alterations in cerebral perfusion pressure.

505
01:07:19,062 --> 01:07:29,400
In nonpregnant individuals, this mechanism protects the brain from hyperperfusion when mean arterial pressures increase up to 160 mm Hg.

506
01:07:29,400 --> 01:07:34,150
These pressures are far greater than those seen in most women with eclampsia.

507
01:07:34,150 --> 01:07:41,225
Thus, to explain eclamptic seizures, it was theorized that autoregulation must be altered by pregnancy.

508
01:07:41,225 --> 01:07:51,262
Some investigators have shown impaired autoregulation in women with preeclampsia (Bergman, 2021b; Janzarik, 2014).

509
01:07:51,262 --> 01:07:58,375
When studied 2 to 3 years postpartum, women who had preeclampsia had returned to normal autoregulation .

510
01:07:58,375 --> 01:08:08,400
Zeeman and associates (2003) showed that cerebral blood flow during the first two trimesters of normal pregnancy is similar to nonpregnant values.

511
01:08:08,400 --> 01:08:13,687
But during the last trimester, flow significantly drops by 20 percent.

512
01:08:13,687 --> 01:08:26,150
They found greater cerebral blood flow in this trimester in women with severe preeclampsia compared with flow in normotensive pregnant women (Lee, 2019; Zeeman, 2004b).

513
01:08:26,150 --> 01:08:36,650
Taken together, these findings suggest that eclampsia occurs when cerebral hyperperfusion forces capillary fluid interstitially because of endothelial damage.

514
01:08:36,650 --> 01:08:39,975
This leak leads to perivascular edema.

515
01:08:39,975 --> 01:08:51,675
Some data suggest that the blood-brain barrier is not impaired, but in-vitro studies may indicate increased permeability (Bergman, 2021a; Burwick, 2018).

516
01:08:51,675 --> 01:08:58,537
Neurological Manifestations Several neurological manifestations typify the preeclampsia syndrome.

517
01:08:58,537 --> 01:09:00,637
Each signifies

518
01:09:00,796 --> 01:09:04,608
severe involvement and requires immediate attention.

519
01:09:04,558 --> 01:09:13,221
First, headache and scotomata are thought to arise from cerebrovascular hyperperfusion that has a predilection for the occipital lobes.

520
01:09:13,221 --> 01:09:25,021
In women preceding an eclamptic convulsion, up to 75 percent have headaches, and 20 to 30 percent have visual changes (Sibai, 2005; Zwart, 2008).

521
01:09:25,021 --> 01:09:28,558
The headaches vary in severity and persistence.

522
01:09:28,558 --> 01:09:38,221
In our experiences, they are unique in that they do not usually respond to traditional analgesia but frequently improve after magnesium sulfate infusion.

523
01:09:38,221 --> 01:09:41,646
Convulsions are diagnostic for eclampsia.

524
01:09:41,646 --> 01:09:46,921
These are caused by abnormal excessive or synchronous neural activity in the brain.

525
01:09:46,921 --> 01:09:53,483
Evidence suggests that extended seizures can cause significant brain injury and later brain dysfunction.

526
01:09:53,483 --> 01:09:58,908
Blindness and generalized cerebral edema are discussed in subsequent sections.

527
01:09:58,908 --> 01:10:07,408
Last, women with eclampsia have been shown to have some cognitive decline when studied 5 to 10 years following the involved pregnancy .

528
01:10:07,408 --> 01:10:12,608
This is discussed further in Chapter 41 (p. 727).

529
01:10:12,608 --> 01:10:24,258
Neuroimaging Studies With CT imaging, localized hypodense lesions are frequently seen with eclampsia at the gray- and white-matter junction and primarily in the parietooccipital lobes.

530
01:10:24,258 --> 01:10:30,346
Frontal and inferior temporal lobes, the basal ganglia, and thalamus are other sites .

531
01:10:30,346 --> 01:10:35,808
These hypodense areas correspond to petechial hemorrhages and local edema.

532
01:10:35,808 --> 01:10:43,008
Edema of the occipital lobes or diffuse cerebral edema may cause blindness, lethargy, and confusion .

533
01:10:43,008 --> 01:10:49,283
Widespread edema can appear as marked compression or even obliteration of the cerebral ventricles.

534
01:10:49,283 --> 01:10:54,808
Such women may develop signs of impending life-threatening transtentorial herniation.

535
01:10:54,808 --> 01:10:59,896
Several MR imaging acquisitions are used to study women with eclampsia .

536
01:10:59,896 --> 01:11:11,158
Common findings are hyperintense T2 lesions in the subcortical and cortical regions of the parietal and occipital lobes, which reflect PRES (see Fig. 40-13).

537
01:11:11,158 --> 01:11:20,496
The basal ganglia, brainstem, and cerebellum are other involved sites (Brewer, 2013; Zeeman, 2004a).

538
01:11:20,496 --> 01:11:34,371
PRES lesions are almost universal in women with eclampsia, and their incidence in women with severe preeclampsia approximates 20 percent (Hosapatna Basavarajappa, 2020; Mayama, 2016).

539
01:11:34,371 --> 01:11:43,171
Although usually reversible, a fourth of these hyperintense lesions with eclampsia have restricted diffusion that signify cerebral infarctions.

540
01:11:43,171 --> 01:11:50,658
These have persistent MR imaging findings (Loureiro, 2003; Zeeman, 2004a).

541
01:11:50,658 --> 01:11:57,808
Visual Changes and Blindness Retinal artery and venular calibers are decreased in women with preeclampsia .

542
01:11:57,808 --> 01:12:08,421
These changes, along with visual cortex involvement, can cause scotomata, blurred vision, or diplopia, which is common with severe preeclampsia and eclampsia.

543
01:12:08,421 --> 01:12:15,258
These symptoms usually improve with magnesium sulfate therapy, or lowered blood pressure, or both.

544
01:12:15,258 --> 01:12:22,346
Blindness is rare with preeclampsia alone, but it complicates up to 15 percent of women with eclampsia .

545
01:12:22,346 --> 01:12:25,858
It can develop a week or more following delivery.

546
01:12:25,858 --> 01:12:30,971
Blindness is usually reversible and may arise from three potential areas.

547
01:12:30,971 --> 01:12:37,433
These are the occipital lobe’s visual cortex, the lateral geniculate nuclei, and the retina.

548
01:12:37,433 --> 01:12:41,158
Occipital blindness is also called amaurosis.

549
01:12:41,158 --> 01:12:48,233
With MR imaging, affected women usually have evidence of extensive occipital lobe vasogenic edema.

550
01:12:48,233 --> 01:12:57,758
Of 15 women cared for at Parkland Hospital, occipital blindness lasted from 4 hours to 8 days, but it resolved completely in all cases .

551
01:12:57,758 --> 01:13:04,121
Rarely, extensive cerebral infarctions may result in total or partial visual defects.

552
01:13:04,121 --> 01:13:09,433
In the retina, ischemia, infarction, or serous detachment may occur .

553
01:13:09,433 --> 01:13:13,971
Retinal infarction, termed Purtscher retinopathy, is rare .

554
01:13:13,971 --> 01:13:19,958
Serous retinal detachment is usually unilateral and seldom causes total visual loss.

555
01:13:19,958 --> 01:13:25,508
Asymptomatic serous retinal detachment is relatively common with preeclampsia .

556
01:13:25,508 --> 01:13:31,871
In most cases of eclampsiaassociated blindness, visual acuity subsequently improves .

557
01:13:31,871 --> 01:13:37,633
If blindness is caused by retinal artery occlusion, vision may be permanently impaired .

558
01:13:37,633 --> 01:13:40,208
Figure 40-14.

559
01:13:40,208 --> 01:13:46,321
Purtscher retinopathy caused by choroidal ischemia and infarction in preeclampsia syndrome.

560
01:13:46,321 --> 01:13:50,546
Ophthalmoscopy shows scattered yellowish, opaque lesions

561
01:13:50,716 --> 01:13:53,116
of the retina (arrows).

562
01:13:53,066 --> 01:13:59,316
Reproduced with permission from Lam, DS, Chan W: Images in clinical medicine.

563
01:13:59,316 --> 01:14:02,491
Choroidal ischemia in preeclampsia.

564
01:14:02,491 --> 01:14:08,253
N Engl J Med 344(10):739, 2001).

565
01:14:08,253 --> 01:14:14,316
Cerebral Edema Manifestations that suggest widespread cerebral edema are worrisome.

566
01:14:14,316 --> 01:14:23,491
During 13 years at Parkland Hospital, 10 of 175 women with eclampsia were diagnosed with symptomatic cerebral edema .

567
01:14:23,491 --> 01:14:29,503
Symptoms ranged from lethargy, confusion, and blurred vision to obtundation and coma.

568
01:14:29,503 --> 01:14:33,078
In most cases, symptoms waxed and waned.

569
01:14:33,078 --> 01:14:39,553
Of these 10, three became comatose and had imaging findings of transtentorial herniation.

570
01:14:39,553 --> 01:14:41,541
One woman died.

571
01:14:41,541 --> 01:14:48,766
Mental status changes generally correlated with the degree of involvement seen with CT and MR imaging studies.

572
01:14:48,766 --> 01:14:57,853
These women are very susceptible to sudden and severe blood pressure elevations, which can acutely worsen the already widespread vasogenic edema.

573
01:14:57,853 --> 01:15:01,591
Thus, careful blood pressure control is essential.

574
01:15:01,591 --> 01:15:04,653
# Uteroplacental Perfusion.

575
01:15:04,653 --> 01:15:15,178
Compromised uteroplacental perfusion is almost certainly a major contributor to the increased perinatal morbidity and mortality rates associated with preeclampsia syndrome .

576
01:15:15,178 --> 01:15:22,578
Contributing defects in endovascular trophoblastic invasion were discussed earlier (p. 692).

577
01:15:22,578 --> 01:15:29,003
Thus, measurement of uterine, intervillous, and placental blood flow would likely be informative.

578
01:15:29,003 --> 01:15:32,478
Attempts to assess these in humans have been hampered.

579
01:15:32,478 --> 01:15:41,641
Barriers include the placenta’s inaccessibility, the complexity of its venous effluent, and the need for invasive techniques or radioisotopes.

580
01:15:41,641 --> 01:15:50,166
As a surrogate, sonographic measurement of uterine artery blood flow velocity can estimate resistance to uteroplacental blood flow.

581
01:15:50,166 --> 01:15:57,241
Vascular resistance is estimated by comparing arterial systolic and diastolic velocity waveforms .

582
01:15:57,241 --> 01:16:11,741
By the completion of placentation, impedance to uterine artery blood flow is markedly decreased, but with abnormal placentation, abnormally high resistance persists (Everett, 2012; Napolitano, 2012).

583
01:16:11,741 --> 01:16:21,066
In earlier studies, peak systolic:diastolic velocity ratios from uterine and umbilical arteries in preeclamptic pregnancies were measured.

584
01:16:21,066 --> 01:16:28,991
In some cases, but not all, resistance was higher (Ferrazzi, 2018; Trudinger, 1990).

585
01:16:28,991 --> 01:16:37,878
Another Doppler waveform, termed uterine artery “notching,” has been linked with increased risks for preeclampsia or fetal-growth restriction .

586
01:16:37,878 --> 01:16:45,903
However, in one MFMU Network study, notching had a low predictive value except for early-onset, severe disease .

587
01:16:45,903 --> 01:16:50,328
Resistance in uterine spiral arteries also has been measured.

588
01:16:50,328 --> 01:16:58,328
In one study, mean resistance values were greater in all women with preeclampsia compared with those in normotensive controls .

589
01:16:58,328 --> 01:17:09,566
Another study used MR imaging and other techniques to assess placental perfusion ex vivo in the myometrial arteries removed from women with preeclampsia or fetal-growth restriction .

590
01:17:09,566 --> 01:17:15,553
In both conditions, myometrial arteries exhibited similar vascular responses.

591
01:17:15,553 --> 01:17:24,441
Despite these findings, evidence for compromised uteroplacental circulation is found in only a few women who later develop preeclampsia.

592
01:17:24,441 --> 01:17:33,703
Indeed, when preeclampsia develops during the third trimester, only a third of women with severe disease have abnormal uterine artery velocimetry .

593
01:17:33,703 --> 01:17:41,141
In a study of 50 women with HELLP syndrome, only a third had abnormal uterine artery waveforms .

594
01:17:41,141 --> 01:17:50,741
In general, the extent of abnormal waveforms correlates with severity of fetal involvement (Ghidini, 2008; Groom, 2009).

595
01:17:50,741 --> 01:18:04,203
These sonographic findings have value for prediction of fetal-growth restriction but not preeclampsia (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2019b; Demers, 2019).

596
01:18:04,203 --> 01:18:10,103
Several other flow velocity waveforms have been investigated for preeclampsia prediction.

597
01:18:10,103 --> 01:18:17,466
However, none is suitable for clinical use (De Kat, 2019; Townsend, 2018).

598
01:18:17,466 --> 01:18:26,816
Fetal-growth Restriction Discussed in detail in Chapter 47, this potential consequence of preeclampsia can serve as one severity indicator .

599
01:18:26,816 --> 01:18:33,816
Namely, poor growth is usually confined to fetuses of women destined to develop severe preeclampsia .

600
01:18:33,816 --> 01:18:42,641
Perry and colleagues (2020) reported that pregnancies complicated by fetal-growth restriction more likely had maternal hemodynamic

601
01:18:42,796 --> 01:18:45,783
indices similar to preeclampsia.

602
01:18:45,733 --> 01:18:56,333
Measures showed higher mean arterial pressure, greater systemic vascular resistance, lower cardiac output, and elevated uterine artery pulsatility index.

603
01:18:56,333 --> 01:19:03,021
Fetuses born to preeclamptic mothers have cardiac remodeling similar to growth-restricted fetuses .

604
01:19:03,021 --> 01:19:04,671
Prediction.

605
01:19:04,671 --> 01:19:12,471
Some biological markers implicated in the genesis of the preeclampsia syndrome have been measured to help predict its development.

606
01:19:12,471 --> 01:19:20,608
Although most have been evaluated in the first half of pregnancy, some have been tested as predictors of severity in the third trimester.

607
01:19:20,608 --> 01:19:25,008
Still others have been used to forecast recurrent preeclampsia.

608
01:19:25,008 --> 01:19:38,508
Overall, these efforts have resulted in testing strategies with poor sensitivity and poor positive predictive values for preeclampsia (Conde-Agudelo, 2015; De Kat, 2019).

609
01:19:38,508 --> 01:19:45,283
Currently, no screening tests for preeclampsia are predictably reliable, valid, and economical.

610
01:19:45,283 --> 01:20:02,608
Because combinations of tests and risk factors might be superior to single predictors, some have developed multivariable screening algorithms (Boutin, 2021; Brunelli, 2020; Copel, 2020; Serra, 2020; Snell, 2020).

611
01:20:02,608 --> 01:20:09,058
One firsttrimester screening protocol using serum sFlt-1 levels has been proposed .

612
01:20:09,058 --> 01:20:17,596
Other examples are midpregnancy algorithms (Black, 2020; Peguero, 2021; Stepan, 2020).

613
01:20:17,596 --> 01:20:22,871
None has been adequately verified sufficiently for widespread clinical use .

614
01:20:22,871 --> 01:20:27,446
# Vascular Resistance Testing and Placental Perfusion.

615
01:20:27,446 --> 01:20:33,058
Most tests in this category are cumbersome, time consuming, and inaccurate.

616
01:20:33,058 --> 01:20:40,171
To evaluate blood pressure changes, three tests assess the blood pressure rise in response to a stimulus.

617
01:20:40,171 --> 01:20:48,583
In one, women at 28 to 32 weeks’ gestation rest in the left lateral decubitus position and then roll to lie supine.

618
01:20:48,583 --> 01:20:55,396
With this roll-over test, rising blood pressure in response to the maneuver signifies a positive test.

619
01:20:55,396 --> 01:21:00,983
The isometric exercise test employs the same principle by squeezing a handball.

620
01:21:00,983 --> 01:21:09,821
The angiotensin II infusion test provides incrementally higher doses intravenously, and the hypertensive response is quantified.

621
01:21:09,821 --> 01:21:18,271
Sensitivities of all three tests range from 55 to 70 percent, and specificities approximated 85 percent .

622
01:21:18,271 --> 01:21:25,608
Uterine artery Doppler velocimetry is posited to reflect faulty trophoblastic invasion of the spiral arteries.

623
01:21:25,608 --> 01:21:33,658
The poor predictive value of this for preeclampsia was described in the Uteroplacental Perfusion section (p. 702).

624
01:21:33,658 --> 01:21:37,471
# Fetal-Placental Unit Endocrine Function.

625
01:21:37,471 --> 01:21:42,383
Several serum analytes have been proposed to help predict preeclampsia.

626
01:21:42,383 --> 01:21:45,233
Newer ones are continually added.

627
01:21:45,233 --> 01:21:50,721
In general, none of these tests is clinically beneficial for hypertension prediction.

628
01:21:50,721 --> 01:21:53,508
# Renal Function Tests.

629
01:21:53,508 --> 01:22:03,783
Hyperuricemia results from reduced uric acid clearance caused by diminished glomerular filtration, increased tubular reabsorption, and decreased secretion.

630
01:22:03,783 --> 01:22:15,496
In one study, the sensitivity of serum uric acid levels to detect preeclampsia ranged from 0 to 55 percent, and specificity was 77 to 95 percent .

631
01:22:15,496 --> 01:22:19,121
These are seldom used to diagnose preeclampsia .

632
01:22:19,121 --> 01:22:28,421
Isolated gestational proteinuria is a risk factor for preeclampsia (Jayaballa, 2015; Morgan, 2016).

633
01:22:28,421 --> 01:22:39,958
But, as a predictive test for preeclampsia, microalbuminuria has sensitivities ranging from 7 to 90 percent and specificities spanning 29 to 97 percent .

634
01:22:39,958 --> 01:22:44,208
# Endothelial Dysfunction and Oxidative Stress.

635
01:22:44,208 --> 01:22:50,983
Endothelial activation and inflammation are major participants in preeclampsia pathophysiology.

636
01:22:50,983 --> 01:22:58,046
Levels of some implicated compounds are elevated in the blood of affected women and have been assessed as predictors.

637
01:22:58,046 --> 01:23:07,046
Fibronectins are high-molecular-weight glycoproteins released from endothelial cells and extracellular matrix following endothelial injury.

638
01:23:07,046 --> 01:23:15,733
In one systematic review, however, neither cellular nor total fibronectin levels were clinically useful to predict preeclampsia .

639
01:23:15,733 --> 01:23:21,121
Thrombocytopenia and platelet dysfunction are integral features of preeclampsia.

640
01:23:21,121 --> 01:23:26,846
Platelet activation causes their augmented destruction and lower blood concentrations.

641
01:23:26,846 --> 01:23:38,033
Platelet volume is increased because of platelet immaturity, and platelet volume has been described to be an early predictor of preeclampsia (Bellos, 2019; Mayer-Pickel,

642
01:23:38,188 --> 01:23:52,588
2021). Although markers of coagulation activation described earlier (p. 696) are elevated, they substantively overlap with levels in normotensive pregnant women (von Dadelszen, 2018).

643
01:23:52,538 --> 01:24:01,613
Of oxidative stress markers, higher levels of lipid peroxides coupled with decreased antioxidant activity can be seen with preeclampsia.

644
01:24:01,613 --> 01:24:21,388
Other markers are iron, transferrin, and ferritin; resistin; hyperhomocysteinemia; blood lipids; and antioxidants such as ascorbic acid and vitamin E (Christiansen, 2015; Conde-Agudelo, 2015; Mackay, 2012; Mignini, 2005).

645
01:24:21,388 --> 01:24:25,075
However, none has sufficient predictive value.

646
01:24:25,075 --> 01:24:29,675
# Angiogenic and Antiangiogenic Factor Imbalance.

647
01:24:29,675 --> 01:24:38,975
An imbalance in angiogenic and antiangiogenic factors is convincingly linked to preeclampsia pathogenesis (p. 694).

648
01:24:38,975 --> 01:24:45,163
Serum levels of VEGF and PlGF begin to drop before clinical preeclampsia develops.

649
01:24:45,163 --> 01:24:52,800
At the same time, levels of some antiangiogenic factors, such as sFlt-1 and sEng, begin to rise.

650
01:24:52,800 --> 01:24:59,650
Factor levels and ratios differ significantly between women with preeclampsia and those who are normotensive.

651
01:24:59,650 --> 01:25:12,350
These show especially good predictive performance with early-onset preeclampsia (Burton, 2019; Cerdeira, 2019; Phipps, 2019; Stepan, 2020).

652
01:25:12,350 --> 01:25:20,463
These tests also can serve as diagnostic adjuncts (Duhig, 2019; Zeisler, 2016).

653
01:25:20,463 --> 01:25:33,050
First, they may aid differentiating between preeclampsia and mimics that include chronic hypertension, chronic kidney disease, systemic lupus erythematosus, and immunological thrombocytopenia.

654
01:25:33,050 --> 01:25:37,763
These tests can also help differentiate mild and severe disease.

655
01:25:37,763 --> 01:25:44,538
These plus other multiple markers will likely have a future role in first-trimester preeclampsia screening .

656
01:25:44,538 --> 01:25:46,925
# Other Markers.

657
01:25:46,925 --> 01:25:53,725
Cell-free DNA (cfDNA) of placental origin can be detected in maternal plasma .

658
01:25:53,725 --> 01:26:01,550
It is hypothesized that cfDNA is released in preeclampsia by accelerated apoptosis of cytotrophoblasts.

659
01:26:01,550 --> 01:26:10,088
However, one MFMU Network study found no correlation between total cfDNA levels and preeclampsia prediction .

660
01:26:10,088 --> 01:26:26,163
Other investigated markers include glycosylated hemoglobin A1c, serum cystatin-c, and first-trimester estimated placental volume (Bellos, 2019; Cavero-Redondo, 2018; Kim, 2021).

661
01:26:26,163 --> 01:26:35,413
Proteomic, metabolomic, and transcriptomic technologies can be employed to study serum and urinary proteins and cellular metabolites.

662
01:26:35,413 --> 01:26:43,788
Preliminary studies indicate their potential predictive value (Bahado-Singh, 2013; Ma, 2014).

663
01:26:43,788 --> 01:26:45,438
Prevention.

664
01:26:45,438 --> 01:26:51,613
Various strategies used to prevent or modify preeclampsia severity have been evaluated.

665
01:26:51,613 --> 01:26:54,825
Some are listed in Table 40-4.

666
01:26:54,825 --> 01:27:00,713
With the possible exception of aspirin, none is convincingly and reproducibly effective.

667
01:27:00,713 --> 01:27:02,950
Table 40-4.

668
01:27:02,950 --> 01:27:11,438
Some Methods to Prevent Preeclampsia That Have Been Evaluated in Randomized Trials # Dietary and Lifestyle Modifications.

669
01:27:11,438 --> 01:27:17,225
A low-salt diet was one of the earliest researched preventions but is not supported by data .

670
01:27:17,225 --> 01:27:24,875
Of studies, one randomized trial showed that a sodium-restricted diet was ineffective in preventing preeclampsia .

671
01:27:24,875 --> 01:27:35,050
Regular exercise during pregnancy is linked to a lower risk of developing preeclampsia (Barakat, 2016; Morris, 2017).

672
01:27:35,050 --> 01:27:41,113
In one systematic review, a similar trend toward risk reduction with exercise was noted .

673
01:27:41,113 --> 01:27:44,175
Only a few studies have been randomized.

674
01:27:44,175 --> 01:27:54,563
Somewhat related, one retrospective cohort study of 677 nonhypertensive women were hospitalized for bed rest because of threatened preterm delivery .

675
01:27:54,563 --> 01:28:07,563
When outcomes of these women were compared with those of the general obstetrical population, bed rest was associated with a significantly reduced risk of developing preeclampsia (relative risk 0.27).

676
01:28:07,563 --> 01:28:19,613
From two small randomized trials, prophylactic bed rest for 4 to 6 hours daily at home was successful in significantly lowering the preeclampsia incidence in women with normal blood pressures .

677
01:28:19,613 --> 01:28:30,713
Currently, the Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine (2020) does not recommend reduced activity for women with hypertensive disorders or for prevention of preeclampsia.

678
01:28:30,713 --> 01:28:43,938
Calcium supplementation has been studied, but in one trial of more than 4500 low-risk nulliparas, supplementation failed to decrease the risk for preeclampsia or pregnancy-associated hypertension .

679
01:28:43,938 --> 01:28:46,188
Similar findings were

680
01:28:46,348 --> 01:28:48,785
reported from another trial .

681
01:28:48,735 --> 01:29:00,785
In aggregate, most trials show that unless women are calcium deficient, supplementation offers no benefits (Palacios, 2019; Sanchez-Ramos, 2017).

682
01:29:00,785 --> 01:29:06,298
Subclinical hypothyroidism is associated with increased preeclampsia risk.

683
01:29:06,298 --> 01:29:17,235
This insufficiency has been postulated to stem from iodine deficiency, but a recent metaanalysis found no association between iodine sufficiency and preeclampsia .

684
01:29:17,235 --> 01:29:21,185
Folic acid was evaluated in a randomized trial .

685
01:29:21,185 --> 01:29:28,423
Nearly 2500 high-risk women were given a 4mg folic acid dose daily or placebo.

686
01:29:28,423 --> 01:29:33,610
The incidence of preeclampsia in both groups approximated 14 percent.

687
01:29:33,610 --> 01:29:40,698
Cardioprotective fatty acids found in some fish likely prevent inflammation-mediated atherogenesis.

688
01:29:40,698 --> 01:29:45,210
Thus, it was posited that they might also prevent preeclampsia.

689
01:29:45,210 --> 01:29:52,198
However, randomized trials conducted thus far show no such benefits from fish oil supplementation .

690
01:29:52,198 --> 01:30:01,210
In one longitudinal cohort study, a seafood diet, compared with a western one, provided a protective effect against preeclampsia .

691
01:30:01,210 --> 01:30:04,210
# Antihypertensive Drugs.

692
01:30:04,210 --> 01:30:15,610
Because of the prior purported benefits of sodium restriction for preeclampsia prevention, diuretic therapy became popular with the advent of chlorothiazide in 1957 .

693
01:30:15,610 --> 01:30:27,098
In one metaanalysis of nine randomized trials with more than 7000 pregnancies, women given diuretics had a lower incidence of edema and hypertension but not of preeclampsia .

694
01:30:27,098 --> 01:30:39,760
Because women with chronic hypertension are at high risk for preeclampsia, several randomized trials have evaluated various antihypertensive drugs to reduce the incidence of superimposed preeclampsia .

695
01:30:39,760 --> 01:30:48,023
A critical analysis of these trials by Staff and coworkers (2015) failed to demonstrate benefits for this goal.

696
01:30:48,023 --> 01:30:50,548
# Antioxidants.

697
01:30:50,548 --> 01:30:58,335
Data imply that an imbalance between oxidant and antioxidant activity plays a role in preeclampsia pathogenesis.

698
01:30:58,335 --> 01:31:05,710
Thus, naturally occurring antioxidants— vitamins C, D, and E—might reduce such oxidation.

699
01:31:05,710 --> 01:31:17,160
Several randomized studies have assessed antioxidant vitamin supplementation for women at high risk for preeclampsia (Burton, 2019; Villar, 2009).

700
01:31:17,160 --> 01:31:26,898
The Combined Antioxidant and Preeclampsia Prediction Studies (CAPPS) by the MFMU Network included almost 10,000 low-risk nulliparas .

701
01:31:26,898 --> 01:31:34,985
None of these studies showed reduced preeclampsia rates in women supplemented with vitamins C and E compared with those given placebo.

702
01:31:34,985 --> 01:31:43,935
Statins were proposed to prevent preeclampsia because they stimulate heme oxygenase-1 expression, which inhibits sF1t-1 release.

703
01:31:43,935 --> 01:31:49,848
Early animal data suggest that statins may prevent hypertensive disorders of pregnancy .

704
01:31:49,848 --> 01:31:57,673
The MFMU Network plans a randomized trial to test pravastatin for prevention, and a pilot study is completed .

705
01:31:57,673 --> 01:32:05,598
Metformin inhibits hypoxic inducible factor 1α by lowering mitochondrial electron transport chain activity.

706
01:32:05,598 --> 01:32:12,160
It reduces sFlt-1 and sEng activity and thus has potential to prevent preeclampsia .

707
01:32:12,160 --> 01:32:23,123
In a preliminary study, prediabetic women were given metformin or placebo throughout pregnancy, and metformin-treated women had a lower incidence of severe preeclampsia .

708
01:32:23,123 --> 01:32:26,435
However, other clinical studies are lacking.

709
01:32:26,435 --> 01:32:29,460
# Antithrombotic Agents.

710
01:32:29,460 --> 01:32:40,185
Preeclampsia is characterized by vasospasm, endothelial cell dysfunction, inflammation, and activation of platelets and the coagulation–hemostasis system.

711
01:32:40,185 --> 01:32:45,535
Other sequelae include placental infarction and spiral artery thrombosis .

712
01:32:45,535 --> 01:32:50,973
Thus, antithrombotic agents have been evaluated to prevent preeclampsia.

713
01:32:50,973 --> 01:32:55,473
Low-molecular-weight heparin has been studied in randomized trials.

714
01:32:55,473 --> 01:33:08,960
In a subsequent metaanalysis using individual data from 963 women, the risk for recurrent preeclampsia, abruption, or fetal-growth restriction was similar in women receiving heparin or placebo .

715
01:33:08,960 --> 01:33:19,035
Low-dose Aspirin In low doses of 50 to 150 mg daily, aspirin effectively inhibits platelet thromboxane A2 biosynthesis.

716
01:33:19,035 --> 01:33:23,235
It has minimal effects on vascular prostacyclin production.

717
01:33:23,235 --> 01:33:29,260
Still, several clinical trials have shown limited benefits in preeclampsia prevention.

718
01:33:29,260 --> 01:33:37,498
In a randomized trial from the MFMU Network, risks for adverse outcomes were not significantly reduced with aspirin therapy .

719
01:33:37,498 --> 01:33:40,348
This study was followed by numerous

720
01:33:40,516 --> 01:33:43,453
similar studies and metaanalyses.

721
01:33:43,403 --> 01:33:57,728
In another randomized trial of more than 1600 women at high risk for preterm preeclampsia, oral low-dose aspirin was given daily from 11 to 14 weeks’ gestation until 36 weeks to prevent recurrence .

722
01:33:57,728 --> 01:34:06,553
The rate of preterm preeclampsia recurrence was 1.6 percent in the aspirin group compared with 4.3 percent in the placebo arm.

723
01:34:06,553 --> 01:34:23,416
In a metaanalysis, Roberge and colleagues (2017) found that aspirin prophylaxis initiated before 16 weeks’ gestation was associated with a significant risk reduction— approximately 60 percent—of preeclampsia and fetal-growth restriction.

724
01:34:23,416 --> 01:34:36,441
At the same time, however, Meher and associates (2017) performed an individual participant data metaanalysis and reported a much lower— approximately 10 percent—risk reduction.

725
01:34:36,441 --> 01:34:42,853
Effects were significant whether therapy was initiated before or after 16 weeks’ gestation.

726
01:34:42,853 --> 01:34:57,653
In a subsequent metaanalysis, Roberge and coworkers (2018) found that aspirin prophylaxis given starting ≤16 weeks’ gestation reduced the risk of preterm, but not term, preeclampsia.

727
01:34:57,653 --> 01:35:06,591
Turner and colleagues (2020) reported that aspirin improved some perinatal outcomes independent of effects on preeclampsia risk.

728
01:35:06,591 --> 01:35:17,153
But to the contrary, the review by Chaemsaithong and coworkers (2020) found no benefits even when low-dose aspirin was given before 11 weeks’ gestation.

729
01:35:17,153 --> 01:35:28,116
Based on these data, the U.S. Preventive Services Task Force (2021) recommends low-dose aspirin prophylaxis for women at high risk for preeclampsia.

730
01:35:28,116 --> 01:35:42,166
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2018, 2020) now recommends low-dose aspirin be given between 12 and 28 weeks’ gestation to help prevent preeclampsia in high-risk women.

731
01:35:42,166 --> 01:35:54,928
Candidates include those with ≥1 of the following: prior preeclampsia, chronic hypertension, overt diabetes, renal disease, autoimmune disorders, and multifetal gestation.

732
01:35:54,928 --> 01:36:10,628
Supplementation may be considered for those with more than one of these qualities: nulliparous, aged older than 35 years, obese, family history of preeclampsia, vulnerable sociodemographics, and prior low-birthweight or growth-restricted neonate.

733
01:36:10,628 --> 01:36:16,691
These results have also raised the question as to whether all pregnant women should be given aspirin .

734
01:36:16,691 --> 01:36:23,478
Low-dose aspirin coupled with heparin mitigates thrombotic sequelae in women with lupus anticoagulant .

735
01:36:23,478 --> 01:36:35,191
Because of a similarly high prevalence of placental thrombotic lesions found with severe preeclampsia, trials have assessed the possible merits of such treatments for women with prior preeclampsia.

736
01:36:35,191 --> 01:36:48,928
In two randomized trials, women with a history of early-onset preeclampsia were given aspirin alone or a regimen of enoxaparin plus aspirin (Groom, 2017; Haddad, 2016).

737
01:36:48,928 --> 01:36:51,078
Outcomes were similar.

738
01:36:51,078 --> 01:37:01,966
Low-molecular-weight heparin, with or without aspirin, may decrease the risk for preeclampsia in high-risk women (de Vries, 2012; Wang, 2020).

